window.jikDocsSearchIndex = [{"title":"Jik Docs","heading":"Jik Docs","url":"index.html#jik-docs","text":"Jik Docs This site contains the Jik language overview, grammar reference, and standard library documentation. Start with the language overview For command-line usage, see the Jik CLI reference For standard library ( jiklib ), see the standard library documentation For syntax definition, see the grammar reference For current implementation limitations, see known issues and current limitations For repository layout, development, and the roadmap, see development notes"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: Commands","heading":"Commands","url":"cli.html#commands","text":"Commands jik run <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C, compile it with the selected C compiler, run the executable, and then remove the executable. Options: --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. --ccflags <args> : Extra C compiler arguments. --release : Build with release-oriented host compiler flags. --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik run hello.jik --cc clang jik build <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C and compile it to an executable. Options: --out <filepath> : Write the executable to this path. When omitted, the default output path is <input_file>.exe on Windows and <input_file> on Linux. --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. --ccflags <args> : Extra C compiler arguments. --preview : Print the compiler command without running it or creating an executable. --release : Build with release-oriented host compiler flags. --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik build hello.jik --release To inspect the compiler command without running it: jik build hello.jik --preview Reachable modules may contain native build directives such as @includedir , @libdir , @link , and @copy . Active directives are applied transitively by build and run ; copied files are placed beside the produced executable. @platform(windows) , @platform(linux) , and @platform(all) select the platform for subsequent build directives in the same source file. @profile(windows-x64-mingw) and @profile(linux-x64-gnu) additionally restrict subsequent native directives to a compatible compiler target; @profile(all) removes that restriction. Profile matching is checked before the host compiler is invoked. MSVC is not currently a supported native build profile. jik tran <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C. Options: --out <filepath> : Write the generated C to this path. When omitted, the default output path is <input-basename>.c . --embed-core : Embed the Jik support library into the generated translation. --emit-source-loc : Emit // source: path:line:column comments before generated declarations that correspond to Jik source declarations. --format-c : Format the generated C file in place with clang-format . --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Generated C includes the Jik support header as #include \"core.h\" . For manual compilation, include the support include directory from jik env 's core_include=<path> value, or use --embed-core to write a self-contained C translation. Example: jik tran hello.jik --embed-core jik check <filepath> Parse and analyze a Jik source file. Options: --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik check hello.jik jik memchk <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C, compile it with debug flags, and run valgrind memcheck. Options: --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. Example: jik memchk hello.jik --cc gcc jik env Print resolved Jik configuration values as key=value lines, including the Jik version, platform, root directory, jiklib path, package path, support include path, selected C compiler, its reported target, and Jik's normalized compiler profile. Example: jik env jik version Print the Jik version. Example: jik version jik help [topic] Show general CLI help, or help for a command, language topic, standard library module, or standard library member. Examples: jik help jik help run jik help vector jik help jik/string jik help jik/io Documentation topic keys are defined in docs/help-index.txt . jik help topics prints the available documentation keys."},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik run <filepath>","heading":"jik run <filepath>","url":"cli.html#jik-run","text":"jik run <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C, compile it with the selected C compiler, run the executable, and then remove the executable. Options: --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. --ccflags <args> : Extra C compiler arguments. --release : Build with release-oriented host compiler flags. --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik run hello.jik --cc clang"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik build <filepath>","heading":"jik build <filepath>","url":"cli.html#jik-build","text":"jik build <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C and compile it to an executable. Options: --out <filepath> : Write the executable to this path. When omitted, the default output path is <input_file>.exe on Windows and <input_file> on Linux. --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. --ccflags <args> : Extra C compiler arguments. --preview : Print the compiler command without running it or creating an executable. --release : Build with release-oriented host compiler flags. --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik build hello.jik --release To inspect the compiler command without running it: jik build hello.jik --preview Reachable modules may contain native build directives such as @includedir , @libdir , @link , and @copy . Active directives are applied transitively by build and run ; copied files are placed beside the produced executable. @platform(windows) , @platform(linux) , and @platform(all) select the platform for subsequent build directives in the same source file. @profile(windows-x64-mingw) and @profile(linux-x64-gnu) additionally restrict subsequent native directives to a compatible compiler target; @profile(all) removes that restriction. Profile matching is checked before the host compiler is invoked. MSVC is not currently a supported native build profile."},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik tran <filepath>","heading":"jik tran <filepath>","url":"cli.html#jik-tran","text":"jik tran <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C. Options: --out <filepath> : Write the generated C to this path. When omitted, the default output path is <input-basename>.c . --embed-core : Embed the Jik support library into the generated translation. --emit-source-loc : Emit // source: path:line:column comments before generated declarations that correspond to Jik source declarations. --format-c : Format the generated C file in place with clang-format . --unsafe-no-bounds-checks : Disable runtime vector bounds checks --region-stats : Print runtime region statistics at program exit. --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Generated C includes the Jik support header as #include \"core.h\" . For manual compilation, include the support include directory from jik env 's core_include=<path> value, or use --embed-core to write a self-contained C translation. Example: jik tran hello.jik --embed-core"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik check <filepath>","heading":"jik check <filepath>","url":"cli.html#jik-check","text":"jik check <filepath> Parse and analyze a Jik source file. Options: --verbose : Print detailed pipeline status information. Example: jik check hello.jik"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik memchk <filepath>","heading":"jik memchk <filepath>","url":"cli.html#jik-memchk","text":"jik memchk <filepath> Translate a Jik source file to C, compile it with debug flags, and run valgrind memcheck. Options: --cc <compiler_name> : C compiler to use. Example: jik memchk hello.jik --cc gcc"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik env","heading":"jik env","url":"cli.html#jik-env","text":"jik env Print resolved Jik configuration values as key=value lines, including the Jik version, platform, root directory, jiklib path, package path, support include path, selected C compiler, its reported target, and Jik's normalized compiler profile. Example: jik env"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik version","heading":"jik version","url":"cli.html#jik-version","text":"jik version Print the Jik version. Example: jik version"},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: jik help [topic]","heading":"jik help [topic]","url":"cli.html#jik-help-topic","text":"jik help [topic] Show general CLI help, or help for a command, language topic, standard library module, or standard library member. Examples: jik help jik help run jik help vector jik help jik/string jik help jik/io Documentation topic keys are defined in docs/help-index.txt . jik help topics prints the available documentation keys."},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: Compiler Selection","heading":"Compiler Selection","url":"cli.html#compiler-selection","text":"Compiler Selection Commands that compile generated C select the host C compiler in this order: The --cc <compiler_name> option. The JIK_CC environment variable. The host default compiler, when available. If no compiler is found, jik run , jik build , and jik memchk fail before compilation."},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: Package Imports","heading":"Package Imports","url":"cli.html#package-imports","text":"Package Imports Imports under pkg/... are resolved from the directory configured by the JIK_PKG_PATH environment variable. The package entry module: use \"pkg/csv\" resolves to: <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/csv.jik Package submodules resolve below the package src/ directory: use \"pkg/csv/reader\" use \"pkg/csv/utils/foo\" <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/reader.jik <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/utils/foo.jik Use jik env to check the currently resolved value. It is printed as pkg_path=<path> . If JIK_PKG_PATH is not set and a program imports pkg/... , compilation fails with a package path error."},{"title":"Jik CLI Reference: Common Workflows","heading":"Common Workflows","url":"cli.html#common-workflows","text":"Common Workflows Use jik check <file> for a fast parse and semantic check. Use jik run <file> while developing a program. Use jik tran <file> when you want to inspect or format the generated C. Use jik build <file> --release when you want an optimized executable. Use jik env to debug how the CLI resolved paths and compiler settings."},{"title":"Development notes and roadmap: Repository layout","heading":"Repository layout","url":"development.html#repository-layout","text":"Repository layout src/jik/ - C seed compiler source src/bootstrap/ - Jik bootstrap compiler source jiklib/ - Jik standard library modules support/ - Jik support library test/ - test suite docs/ - language and development documentation examples/ - example Jik programs assets/ - repository assets scripts/ - development scripts tools/ - development tools, including syntax highlighting"},{"title":"Development notes and roadmap: Building and testing","heading":"Building and testing","url":"development.html#building-and-testing","text":"Building and testing Build the C seed compiler from the repository root with make . Build the Jik bootstrap compiler as jik1 with make bootstrap . Run the test suite with make test ."},{"title":"Development notes and roadmap: Roadmap","heading":"Roadmap","url":"development.html#roadmap","text":"Roadmap 0.1.x — implementation stabilisation Keep the intended language syntax stable. Find and fix compiler and support-library issues. Extend the standard library where needed. Expand the test suite. Validate the implementation by writing real Jik programs. Later Continue bootstrapping the implementation by rewriting Jik in Jik. Add concurrency support, either in the C implementation or during bootstrapping."},{"title":"Development notes and roadmap: 0.1.x — implementation stabilisation","heading":"0.1.x — implementation stabilisation","url":"development.html#0-1-x-implementation-stabilisation","text":"0.1.x — implementation stabilisation Keep the intended language syntax stable. Find and fix compiler and support-library issues. Extend the standard library where needed. Expand the test suite. Validate the implementation by writing real Jik programs."},{"title":"Development notes and roadmap: Later","heading":"Later","url":"development.html#later","text":"Later Continue bootstrapping the implementation by rewriting Jik in Jik. Add concurrency support, either in the C implementation or during bootstrapping."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Scope","heading":"Scope","url":"grammar.html#scope","text":"Scope This grammar describes syntax, not full typing or region semantics. Some constructs are context-sensitive. In particular: Type.Member may denote enum values or variant tags. x[Tag] may denote variant payload access only after semantic resolution. allocation suffixes such as [r] and [.x] are syntactically regular, but semantically restricted. Newlines are significant between statements and after block headers."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Lexical Structure","heading":"Lexical Structure","url":"grammar.html#lexical-structure","text":"Lexical Structure Comments comment ::= \"//\" { any-char-except-newline } Identifiers identifier ::= letter { letter | digit | \"_\" } | \"_\" qualified_identifier ::= identifier [ \"::\" identifier ] Notes: _ is a reserved standalone token for the local region. Vec , Dict , Option , Some , and None are reserved keywords. Identifiers are currently ASCII-only. Literals digit_sequence ::= digit { digit | \"_\" digit } integer_literal ::= digit_sequence float_literal ::= digit_sequence \".\" digit_sequence | digit_sequence [ \".\" digit_sequence ] exponent exponent ::= (\"e\" | \"E\") [ \"+\" | \"-\" ] digit_sequence char_literal ::= \"'\" char-char \"'\" string_literal ::= '\"' { string-char } '\"' ml_string ::= '\"\"\"' { any-char } '\"\"\"' Notes: The current lexer requires a float to begin with a digit, so 0.5 is valid while .5 is not. Numeric separators ( _ ) are allowed only between digits and are ignored in the value. Character escapes support \\n , \\t , \\r , \\0 , \\xNN , \\\\ , \\' , and \\\" . Ordinary string literals support \\n , \\t , \\r , \\xNN , \\\\ , \\' , and \\\" . Ordinary and multiline string literals may contain UTF-8 text. Source files that contain non-ASCII text should be saved as UTF-8. Ordinary string literals do not support \\0 ; Jik strings are currently null-terminated. String length, indexing, and slicing are byte-based. Embedded C embed_block ::= \"@embed\" \"{\" identifier \"}\" newline { any-line } identifier newline? The closing delimiter must appear alone on its line, apart from trailing whitespace. Embedded C is copied through to the generated C translation. Non-ASCII text in embedded C therefore remains dependent on the host C compiler's handling of C source encoding. Build Directives build_directive ::= platform_directive | profile_directive | path_directive | link_directive platform_directive ::= \"@platform\" \"(\" ( \"all\" | \"windows\" | \"linux\" ) \")\" newline profile_directive ::= \"@profile\" \"(\" ( \"all\" | \"windows-x64-mingw\" | \"linux-x64-gnu\" ) \")\" newline path_directive ::= ( \"@includedir\" | \"@libdir\" | \"@copy\" ) \"(\" string_literal { \",\" string_literal } \")\" newline link_directive ::= \"@link\" \"(\" string_literal { \",\" string_literal } \")\" newline Build directives are top-level declarations. Each physical module starts with platform and profile all . @platform(windows) or @platform(linux) selects the platform for subsequent build directives in that module, while @platform(all) returns to platform-independent requirements. Platform state does not cross module boundaries and does not conditionally compile Jik declarations or embedded C. @profile(windows-x64-mingw) and @profile(linux-x64-gnu) restrict subsequent native build directives to a compatible host C compiler target. @profile(all) removes that restriction. A profile-restricted directive is active only when both its platform and profile match. jik build , jik run , and jik memchk detect the selected compiler target before invoking the compiler and report an incompatible native module early. jik check and jik tran only parse these directives; they do not probe a compiler. MSVC is not currently a supported native build profile. Relative include directories, library directories, and copied files are resolved from the source file containing the directive. Directives from all reachable modules are applied by build , run , and memchk ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Comments","heading":"Comments","url":"grammar.html#comments","text":"Comments comment ::= \"//\" { any-char-except-newline }"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Identifiers","heading":"Identifiers","url":"grammar.html#identifiers","text":"Identifiers identifier ::= letter { letter | digit | \"_\" } | \"_\" qualified_identifier ::= identifier [ \"::\" identifier ] Notes: _ is a reserved standalone token for the local region. Vec , Dict , Option , Some , and None are reserved keywords. Identifiers are currently ASCII-only."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Literals","heading":"Literals","url":"grammar.html#literals","text":"Literals digit_sequence ::= digit { digit | \"_\" digit } integer_literal ::= digit_sequence float_literal ::= digit_sequence \".\" digit_sequence | digit_sequence [ \".\" digit_sequence ] exponent exponent ::= (\"e\" | \"E\") [ \"+\" | \"-\" ] digit_sequence char_literal ::= \"'\" char-char \"'\" string_literal ::= '\"' { string-char } '\"' ml_string ::= '\"\"\"' { any-char } '\"\"\"' Notes: The current lexer requires a float to begin with a digit, so 0.5 is valid while .5 is not. Numeric separators ( _ ) are allowed only between digits and are ignored in the value. Character escapes support \\n , \\t , \\r , \\0 , \\xNN , \\\\ , \\' , and \\\" . Ordinary string literals support \\n , \\t , \\r , \\xNN , \\\\ , \\' , and \\\" . Ordinary and multiline string literals may contain UTF-8 text. Source files that contain non-ASCII text should be saved as UTF-8. Ordinary string literals do not support \\0 ; Jik strings are currently null-terminated. String length, indexing, and slicing are byte-based."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Embedded C","heading":"Embedded C","url":"grammar.html#embedded-c","text":"Embedded C embed_block ::= \"@embed\" \"{\" identifier \"}\" newline { any-line } identifier newline? The closing delimiter must appear alone on its line, apart from trailing whitespace. Embedded C is copied through to the generated C translation. Non-ASCII text in embedded C therefore remains dependent on the host C compiler's handling of C source encoding."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Build Directives","heading":"Build Directives","url":"grammar.html#build-directives","text":"Build Directives build_directive ::= platform_directive | profile_directive | path_directive | link_directive platform_directive ::= \"@platform\" \"(\" ( \"all\" | \"windows\" | \"linux\" ) \")\" newline profile_directive ::= \"@profile\" \"(\" ( \"all\" | \"windows-x64-mingw\" | \"linux-x64-gnu\" ) \")\" newline path_directive ::= ( \"@includedir\" | \"@libdir\" | \"@copy\" ) \"(\" string_literal { \",\" string_literal } \")\" newline link_directive ::= \"@link\" \"(\" string_literal { \",\" string_literal } \")\" newline Build directives are top-level declarations. Each physical module starts with platform and profile all . @platform(windows) or @platform(linux) selects the platform for subsequent build directives in that module, while @platform(all) returns to platform-independent requirements. Platform state does not cross module boundaries and does not conditionally compile Jik declarations or embedded C. @profile(windows-x64-mingw) and @profile(linux-x64-gnu) restrict subsequent native build directives to a compatible host C compiler target. @profile(all) removes that restriction. A profile-restricted directive is active only when both its platform and profile match. jik build , jik run , and jik memchk detect the selected compiler target before invoking the compiler and report an incompatible native module early. jik check and jik tran only parse these directives; they do not probe a compiler. MSVC is not currently a supported native build profile. Relative include directories, library directories, and copied files are resolved from the source file containing the directive. Directives from all reachable modules are applied by build , run , and memchk ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Types","heading":"Types","url":"grammar.html#types","text":"Types type_desc ::= named_type [ alloc_suffix ] | \"Vec\" \"[\" type_desc \"]\" [ alloc_suffix ] | \"Dict\" \"[\" type_desc \"]\" [ alloc_suffix ] | \"Option\" \"[\" type_desc \"]\" [ alloc_suffix ] named_type ::= qualified_identifier Named types include built-in names such as: int double bool char String Region Site They also include user-defined struct , enum , and variant names."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Allocation Suffixes","heading":"Allocation Suffixes","url":"grammar.html#allocation-suffixes","text":"Allocation Suffixes Composite literals and some type descriptions may be followed by an allocation suffix: alloc_suffix ::= \"[\" identifier \"]\" | \"[\" \".\" identifier \"]\" Examples: Point{}[r] Point{}[.p] Some{\"x\"}[r] None[r] The parser also accepts the local region value _ as an ordinary expression, but allocation suffix syntax itself expects either id or .id . Allocation suffix syntax takes precedence immediately after composite literals. As a result, forms like [1, 2][0] are not parsed as direct subscripting of a literal. In the current language, composite temporaries must first be bound to a name before subscripting, member access, mutation, or iteration."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Program Structure","heading":"Program Structure","url":"grammar.html#program-structure","text":"Program Structure program ::= { newline | use_decl | global_decl | top_decl | variant_decl | enum_decl | table_decl | embed_block | build_directive } Imports use_decl ::= \"use\" string_literal [ \"as\" identifier ] Globals global_decl ::= identifier \":=\" expr Top-level typed globals such as x: int are not parsed directly. The current parser accepts only top-level := declarations. Top-Level Declarations top_decl ::= decl_prefix ( func_decl | struct_decl ) decl_prefix ::= { \"extern\" | \"throws\" | \"init\" } Current parser restrictions: extern and throws may prefix func . extern init func ... declares the default initializer for an opaque extern struct return type. init is valid only with extern func . extern may prefix struct . throws struct ... is rejected. Functions func_decl ::= \"func\" identifier \"(\" [ params ] \")\" [ \"->\" type_desc ] \":\" newline block \"end\" Extern functions use a different form: extern_func_decl ::= \"extern\" [ \"init\" | \"throws\" ] \"func\" identifier \"as\" identifier \"(\" [ extern_params ] \")\" \"->\" type_desc Parameters params ::= param { \",\" param } param ::= [ \"foreign\" ] identifier [ \":\" type_desc ] extern_params ::= extern_param { \",\" extern_param } extern_param ::= [ \"foreign\" ] identifier \":\" type_desc Notes: Ordinary function parameters may omit type annotations. Extern function parameters must have type annotations. Structs struct_decl ::= \"struct\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier \":\" type_desc newline } \"end\" extern_struct_decl ::= \"extern\" \"struct\" identifier \"as\" identifier Variants variant_decl ::= \"variant\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier [ \":\" type_desc ] newline } \"end\" Enums enum_decl ::= \"enum\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier newline } \"end\" Tables table_decl ::= \"table\" identifier \"[\" type_desc \"]\" \"->\" type_desc \":\" newline { table_entry } \"end\" table_entry ::= identifier \":\" expr newline The key type must resolve to an enum or variant. Every enum member or variant tag must occur exactly once. A table lookup uses ordinary subscript syntax, such as Names[key] . Table entries cannot be assigned through, contain function calls (including built-ins), or read global variables."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Imports","heading":"Imports","url":"grammar.html#imports","text":"Imports use_decl ::= \"use\" string_literal [ \"as\" identifier ]"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Globals","heading":"Globals","url":"grammar.html#globals","text":"Globals global_decl ::= identifier \":=\" expr Top-level typed globals such as x: int are not parsed directly. The current parser accepts only top-level := declarations."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Top-Level Declarations","heading":"Top-Level Declarations","url":"grammar.html#top-level-declarations","text":"Top-Level Declarations top_decl ::= decl_prefix ( func_decl | struct_decl ) decl_prefix ::= { \"extern\" | \"throws\" | \"init\" } Current parser restrictions: extern and throws may prefix func . extern init func ... declares the default initializer for an opaque extern struct return type. init is valid only with extern func . extern may prefix struct . throws struct ... is rejected."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"grammar.html#functions","text":"Functions func_decl ::= \"func\" identifier \"(\" [ params ] \")\" [ \"->\" type_desc ] \":\" newline block \"end\" Extern functions use a different form: extern_func_decl ::= \"extern\" [ \"init\" | \"throws\" ] \"func\" identifier \"as\" identifier \"(\" [ extern_params ] \")\" \"->\" type_desc"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Parameters","heading":"Parameters","url":"grammar.html#parameters","text":"Parameters params ::= param { \",\" param } param ::= [ \"foreign\" ] identifier [ \":\" type_desc ] extern_params ::= extern_param { \",\" extern_param } extern_param ::= [ \"foreign\" ] identifier \":\" type_desc Notes: Ordinary function parameters may omit type annotations. Extern function parameters must have type annotations."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Structs","heading":"Structs","url":"grammar.html#structs","text":"Structs struct_decl ::= \"struct\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier \":\" type_desc newline } \"end\" extern_struct_decl ::= \"extern\" \"struct\" identifier \"as\" identifier"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Variants","heading":"Variants","url":"grammar.html#variants","text":"Variants variant_decl ::= \"variant\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier [ \":\" type_desc ] newline } \"end\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Enums","heading":"Enums","url":"grammar.html#enums","text":"Enums enum_decl ::= \"enum\" identifier \":\" newline { identifier newline } \"end\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Tables","heading":"Tables","url":"grammar.html#tables","text":"Tables table_decl ::= \"table\" identifier \"[\" type_desc \"]\" \"->\" type_desc \":\" newline { table_entry } \"end\" table_entry ::= identifier \":\" expr newline The key type must resolve to an enum or variant. Every enum member or variant tag must occur exactly once. A table lookup uses ordinary subscript syntax, such as Names[key] . Table entries cannot be assigned through, contain function calls (including built-ins), or read global variables."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Blocks and Statements","heading":"Blocks and Statements","url":"grammar.html#blocks-and-statements","text":"Blocks and Statements block ::= { statement newline } The current parser expects at least one newline after each statement in a block. Statements statement ::= return_stmt | if_stmt | match_stmt | while_stmt | for_stmt | break_stmt | continue_stmt | try_stmt | decl_stmt | assign_stmt | call_stmt | must_call_stmt return_stmt ::= \"return\" [ expr ] break_stmt ::= \"break\" continue_stmt ::= \"continue\" decl_stmt ::= identifier \":=\" expr | identifier \":=\" \"try\" call_expr | identifier \":\" type_desc assign_stmt ::= lvalue assign_op expr assign_op ::= \"=\" | \"+=\" | \"-=\" | \"*=\" | \"/=\" | \"%=\" call_stmt ::= call_expr must_call_stmt ::= \"must\" call_expr Valid lvalues in the parser are: lvalue ::= identifier | primary \".\" identifier | primary \"[\" expr \"]\" If if_stmt ::= \"if\" expr \":\" newline block { \"elif\" expr \":\" newline block } [ \"else\" \":\" newline block ] \"end\" While while_stmt ::= \"while\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" For for_stmt ::= \"for\" identifier \"=\" expr \",\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" | \"for\" identifier \"in\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" | \"for\" identifier \",\" identifier \"in\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" In the two-name form, vectors bind index, value and dictionaries bind key, value . Try / Except try_stmt ::= \"try\" call_expr | \"try\" ( call_expr | identifier \":=\" call_expr ) \":\" newline block \"except\" \":\" newline block \"end\" try f(...) and x := try f(...) propagate a failure from f to the enclosing throws func . Unlike a try ... except ... end declaration, x belongs to the enclosing scope because execution can continue past the declaration only when f succeeds. The call-only propagation form has no trailing colon. A colon starts the local try ... except ... end form. Match match_stmt ::= \"match\" expr \":\" newline { case_clause } \"end\" case_clause ::= \"case\" variant_pattern \":\" newline block | \"case\" enum_pattern \":\" newline block variant_pattern ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier \"{\" identifier \"}\" enum_pattern ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier Current implementation note: case Value.TAG{x}: binds a variant payload, while case Value.TAG: matches a payloadless variant tag or an enum member. The matched value determines whether a qualified case denotes a variant tag or enum member. Enum matches require every member exactly once and do not permit payload bindings."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Statements","heading":"Statements","url":"grammar.html#statements","text":"Statements statement ::= return_stmt | if_stmt | match_stmt | while_stmt | for_stmt | break_stmt | continue_stmt | try_stmt | decl_stmt | assign_stmt | call_stmt | must_call_stmt return_stmt ::= \"return\" [ expr ] break_stmt ::= \"break\" continue_stmt ::= \"continue\" decl_stmt ::= identifier \":=\" expr | identifier \":=\" \"try\" call_expr | identifier \":\" type_desc assign_stmt ::= lvalue assign_op expr assign_op ::= \"=\" | \"+=\" | \"-=\" | \"*=\" | \"/=\" | \"%=\" call_stmt ::= call_expr must_call_stmt ::= \"must\" call_expr Valid lvalues in the parser are: lvalue ::= identifier | primary \".\" identifier | primary \"[\" expr \"]\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: If","heading":"If","url":"grammar.html#if","text":"If if_stmt ::= \"if\" expr \":\" newline block { \"elif\" expr \":\" newline block } [ \"else\" \":\" newline block ] \"end\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: While","heading":"While","url":"grammar.html#while","text":"While while_stmt ::= \"while\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: For","heading":"For","url":"grammar.html#for","text":"For for_stmt ::= \"for\" identifier \"=\" expr \",\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" | \"for\" identifier \"in\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" | \"for\" identifier \",\" identifier \"in\" expr \":\" newline block \"end\" In the two-name form, vectors bind index, value and dictionaries bind key, value ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Try / Except","heading":"Try / Except","url":"grammar.html#try-except","text":"Try / Except try_stmt ::= \"try\" call_expr | \"try\" ( call_expr | identifier \":=\" call_expr ) \":\" newline block \"except\" \":\" newline block \"end\" try f(...) and x := try f(...) propagate a failure from f to the enclosing throws func . Unlike a try ... except ... end declaration, x belongs to the enclosing scope because execution can continue past the declaration only when f succeeds. The call-only propagation form has no trailing colon. A colon starts the local try ... except ... end form."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Match","heading":"Match","url":"grammar.html#match","text":"Match match_stmt ::= \"match\" expr \":\" newline { case_clause } \"end\" case_clause ::= \"case\" variant_pattern \":\" newline block | \"case\" enum_pattern \":\" newline block variant_pattern ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier \"{\" identifier \"}\" enum_pattern ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier Current implementation note: case Value.TAG{x}: binds a variant payload, while case Value.TAG: matches a payloadless variant tag or an enum member. The matched value determines whether a qualified case denotes a variant tag or enum member. Enum matches require every member exactly once and do not permit payload bindings."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Expressions","heading":"Expressions","url":"grammar.html#expressions","text":"Expressions The parser uses the following precedence, from lowest to highest. Ternary expr ::= ternary ternary ::= logical_or [ \"if\" expr \"else\" ternary ] This yields expressions such as: 2 if x == 3 else 12 Logical Operators logical_or ::= logical_and { \"or\" logical_and } logical_and ::= is_expr { \"and\" is_expr } is is_expr ::= comparison [ \"is\" is_target ] is_target ::= \"Some\" | \"None\" | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier Examples: x is Some x is None v is Value.INT Comparison and Arithmetic comparison ::= term { (\"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \">\" | \"<=\" | \">=\") term } term ::= factor { (\"+\" | \"-\") factor } factor ::= unary { (\"*\" | \"/\" | \"%\") unary } unary ::= ( \"not\" | \"-\" | \"must\" ) unary | primary Primary Expressions primary ::= atom { postfix } postfix ::= \".\" identifier [ \"(\" [ args ] \")\" ] | \"[\" expr \"]\" | \"[\" [ expr ] \":\" [ expr ] \"]\" | \"?\" | \"!\" Examples: point.x values[i] values[1:3] values[:3] values[1:] values[:] opt? load_config()! v[Value.INT] Atoms atom ::= integer_literal | float_literal | char_literal | \"true\" | \"false\" | string_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | ml_string [ alloc_suffix ] | \"Some\" \"{\" expr \"}\" [ alloc_suffix ] | \"None\" [ alloc_suffix ] | qualified_identifier | call_expr | struct_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | variant_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | \"(\" expr \")\" | vector_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | dict_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | \".\" identifier | \"_\" Calls call_expr ::= qualified_identifier \"(\" [ args ] \")\" args ::= expr { \",\" expr } receiver.name(args) is a uniform function call. push , pop , len , clear , and copy support this form for any receiver type accepted by the corresponding builtin. Otherwise, receiver must have a struct or variant type and name must be an ordinary function in that type's defining module whose first parameter has an explicit annotation for the receiver's type. It is shorthand for a normal call with receiver as the first argument. Special implementation rule: unwrap(x) is parsed specially and treated as equivalent to x? . Struct Literals struct_literal ::= qualified_identifier \"{\" [ struct_field { \",\" struct_field } ] \"}\" struct_field ::= identifier \"=\" expr Variant Literals variant_literal ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier \"{\" [ expr ] \"}\" Examples: Value.INT{3} Value.TEXT{\"hi\"}[r] Value.INT{} Value.EOF Vector Literals vector_literal ::= \"[\" expr \"of\" expr \"]\" | \"[\" expr { \",\" expr } \"]\" Examples: [10 of 0] [1, 2, 3] Dictionary Literals dict_literal ::= \"{\" [ dict_entry { \",\" dict_entry } [ \",\" ] ] \"}\" dict_entry ::= expr \":\" expr Keys are checked semantically and must be of type String ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Ternary","heading":"Ternary","url":"grammar.html#ternary","text":"Ternary expr ::= ternary ternary ::= logical_or [ \"if\" expr \"else\" ternary ] This yields expressions such as: 2 if x == 3 else 12"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Logical Operators","heading":"Logical Operators","url":"grammar.html#logical-operators","text":"Logical Operators logical_or ::= logical_and { \"or\" logical_and } logical_and ::= is_expr { \"and\" is_expr }"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: is","heading":"is","url":"grammar.html#is","text":"is is_expr ::= comparison [ \"is\" is_target ] is_target ::= \"Some\" | \"None\" | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier Examples: x is Some x is None v is Value.INT"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Comparison and Arithmetic","heading":"Comparison and Arithmetic","url":"grammar.html#comparison-and-arithmetic","text":"Comparison and Arithmetic comparison ::= term { (\"==\" | \"!=\" | \"<\" | \">\" | \"<=\" | \">=\") term } term ::= factor { (\"+\" | \"-\") factor } factor ::= unary { (\"*\" | \"/\" | \"%\") unary } unary ::= ( \"not\" | \"-\" | \"must\" ) unary | primary"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Primary Expressions","heading":"Primary Expressions","url":"grammar.html#primary-expressions","text":"Primary Expressions primary ::= atom { postfix } postfix ::= \".\" identifier [ \"(\" [ args ] \")\" ] | \"[\" expr \"]\" | \"[\" [ expr ] \":\" [ expr ] \"]\" | \"?\" | \"!\" Examples: point.x values[i] values[1:3] values[:3] values[1:] values[:] opt? load_config()! v[Value.INT]"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Atoms","heading":"Atoms","url":"grammar.html#atoms","text":"Atoms atom ::= integer_literal | float_literal | char_literal | \"true\" | \"false\" | string_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | ml_string [ alloc_suffix ] | \"Some\" \"{\" expr \"}\" [ alloc_suffix ] | \"None\" [ alloc_suffix ] | qualified_identifier | call_expr | struct_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | variant_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | \"(\" expr \")\" | vector_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | dict_literal [ alloc_suffix ] | \".\" identifier | \"_\""},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Calls","heading":"Calls","url":"grammar.html#calls","text":"Calls call_expr ::= qualified_identifier \"(\" [ args ] \")\" args ::= expr { \",\" expr } receiver.name(args) is a uniform function call. push , pop , len , clear , and copy support this form for any receiver type accepted by the corresponding builtin. Otherwise, receiver must have a struct or variant type and name must be an ordinary function in that type's defining module whose first parameter has an explicit annotation for the receiver's type. It is shorthand for a normal call with receiver as the first argument. Special implementation rule: unwrap(x) is parsed specially and treated as equivalent to x? ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Struct Literals","heading":"Struct Literals","url":"grammar.html#struct-literals","text":"Struct Literals struct_literal ::= qualified_identifier \"{\" [ struct_field { \",\" struct_field } ] \"}\" struct_field ::= identifier \"=\" expr"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Variant Literals","heading":"Variant Literals","url":"grammar.html#variant-literals","text":"Variant Literals variant_literal ::= qualified_identifier \".\" identifier | qualified_identifier \".\" identifier \"{\" [ expr ] \"}\" Examples: Value.INT{3} Value.TEXT{\"hi\"}[r] Value.INT{} Value.EOF"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Vector Literals","heading":"Vector Literals","url":"grammar.html#vector-literals","text":"Vector Literals vector_literal ::= \"[\" expr \"of\" expr \"]\" | \"[\" expr { \",\" expr } \"]\" Examples: [10 of 0] [1, 2, 3]"},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Dictionary Literals","heading":"Dictionary Literals","url":"grammar.html#dictionary-literals","text":"Dictionary Literals dict_literal ::= \"{\" [ dict_entry { \",\" dict_entry } [ \",\" ] ] \"}\" dict_entry ::= expr \":\" expr Keys are checked semantically and must be of type String ."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Semantic Resolution Notes","heading":"Semantic Resolution Notes","url":"grammar.html#semantic-resolution-notes","text":"Semantic Resolution Notes These forms are parsed first and refined later: EnumName.VALUE is initially parsed as member access, then rewritten as an enum value during semantic analysis. VariantName.TAG is initially parsed as member access, then rewritten during semantic analysis as either a payloadless variant value or a variant tag marker. value[VariantName.TAG] is syntactically a subscript expression and becomes variant payload access only after semantic resolution."},{"title":"Jik Grammar Reference: Current Implementation Gaps and Constraints","heading":"Current Implementation Gaps and Constraints","url":"grammar.html#current-implementation-gaps-and-constraints","text":"Current Implementation Gaps and Constraints Top-level typed globals are not currently parsed. match supports payload-binding and payloadless variant-tag patterns; richer patterns are not part of the current parser."},{"title":"argparse: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#types","text":"Types ArgumentOption Configured option handle. --- Parser Configured parser. --- ParseResult Values returned by parse . Fields positionals: Dict[String] - Values of configured positional arguments, keyed by positional name. options: Dict[Dict[String]] - Present options, keyed by configured long option spelling (including -- ). Each value dictionary is keyed by its declared value name; a flag has an empty dictionary."},{"title":"argparse: ArgumentOption","heading":"ArgumentOption","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#argumentoption","text":"ArgumentOption Configured option handle. ---"},{"title":"argparse: Parser","heading":"Parser","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#parser","text":"Parser Configured parser. ---"},{"title":"argparse: ParseResult","heading":"ParseResult","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#parseresult","text":"ParseResult Values returned by parse . Fields positionals: Dict[String] - Values of configured positional arguments, keyed by positional name. options: Dict[Dict[String]] - Present options, keyed by configured long option spelling (including -- ). Each value dictionary is keyed by its declared value name; a flag has an empty dictionary."},{"title":"argparse: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#functions","text":"Functions add_option(parser: Parser, name: String, short_name: String, help: String) -> ArgumentOption Add an option and return it. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. name: String - Long option name, such as --verbose . Foreign parameter. short_name: String - Short option name, such as -v , or an empty string for a long-only option. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. Returns Configured option handle. Notes An option with no declared values acts as a flag. Long names must begin with -- . Short names are optional; when provided, they must be - followed by one character. Duplicate option names terminate through std::panic . --- add_option_positional(option: ArgumentOption, name: String, help: String) Add a positional value consumed by an option. Parameters option: ArgumentOption - Option returned by add_option . name: String - Value name. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. Notes Value names must be unique within an option because they become keys in the parse result. --- add_positional(parser: Parser, name: String, help: String) Add a required positional argument. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. name: String - Positional argument name. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. --- format_help(parser: Parser, r: Region) -> String Format generated parser help text. Parameters parser: Parser - Configured parser. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Help text. --- new(name: String, r: Region) -> Parser Create an empty argument parser with a program name for generated help. Parameters name: String - Program name shown in the usage line. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the parser and all configured data. Returns Parser --- set_description(parser: Parser, description: String) Set the prose description shown before the generated usage section. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. description: String - Description shown before the usage section. Foreign parameter. --- throws parse(parser: Parser, args: Vec[String], r: Region) -> ParseResult Parse command-line arguments into a result. Parameters parser: Parser - Configured parser. Foreign parameter. args: Vec[String] - Command-line tokens after the executable name. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the returned result. Returns ParseResult Notes args must not include the executable name. The parsed dictionaries are allocated in r . Options may be written with their long or short names. -- ends option parsing. Repeating an option is a parse error. Supported forms are --long and -s ; grouped short options and inline values such as --output=file are not supported. Option values are retrieved by name, such as result.options[\"--output\"]?[\"file\"]? . Unknown or repeated options, missing option values, and missing or extra positional arguments return an argparse: error."},{"title":"argparse: add_option(parser: Parser, name: String, short_name: String, help: String) -> ArgumentOption","heading":"add_option(parser: Parser, name: String, short_name: String, help: String) -> ArgumentOption","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#add-option-parser-parser-name-string-short-name-string-help-string-argumentoption","text":"add_option(parser: Parser, name: String, short_name: String, help: String) -> ArgumentOption Add an option and return it. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. name: String - Long option name, such as --verbose . Foreign parameter. short_name: String - Short option name, such as -v , or an empty string for a long-only option. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. Returns Configured option handle. Notes An option with no declared values acts as a flag. Long names must begin with -- . Short names are optional; when provided, they must be - followed by one character. Duplicate option names terminate through std::panic . ---"},{"title":"argparse: add_option_positional(option: ArgumentOption, name: String, help: String)","heading":"add_option_positional(option: ArgumentOption, name: String, help: String)","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#add-option-positional-option-argumentoption-name-string-help-string","text":"add_option_positional(option: ArgumentOption, name: String, help: String) Add a positional value consumed by an option. Parameters option: ArgumentOption - Option returned by add_option . name: String - Value name. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. Notes Value names must be unique within an option because they become keys in the parse result. ---"},{"title":"argparse: add_positional(parser: Parser, name: String, help: String)","heading":"add_positional(parser: Parser, name: String, help: String)","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#add-positional-parser-parser-name-string-help-string","text":"add_positional(parser: Parser, name: String, help: String) Add a required positional argument. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. name: String - Positional argument name. Foreign parameter. help: String - Help text. Foreign parameter. ---"},{"title":"argparse: format_help(parser: Parser, r: Region) -> String","heading":"format_help(parser: Parser, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#format-help-parser-parser-r-region-string","text":"format_help(parser: Parser, r: Region) -> String Format generated parser help text. Parameters parser: Parser - Configured parser. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Help text. ---"},{"title":"argparse: new(name: String, r: Region) -> Parser","heading":"new(name: String, r: Region) -> Parser","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#new-name-string-r-region-parser","text":"new(name: String, r: Region) -> Parser Create an empty argument parser with a program name for generated help. Parameters name: String - Program name shown in the usage line. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the parser and all configured data. Returns Parser ---"},{"title":"argparse: set_description(parser: Parser, description: String)","heading":"set_description(parser: Parser, description: String)","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#set-description-parser-parser-description-string","text":"set_description(parser: Parser, description: String) Set the prose description shown before the generated usage section. Parameters parser: Parser - Parser to configure. description: String - Description shown before the usage section. Foreign parameter. ---"},{"title":"argparse: throws parse(parser: Parser, args: Vec[String], r: Region) -> ParseResult","heading":"throws parse(parser: Parser, args: Vec[String], r: Region) -> ParseResult","url":"jiklib/argparse.html#throws-parse-parser-parser-args-vec-string-r-region-parseresult","text":"throws parse(parser: Parser, args: Vec[String], r: Region) -> ParseResult Parse command-line arguments into a result. Parameters parser: Parser - Configured parser. Foreign parameter. args: Vec[String] - Command-line tokens after the executable name. Foreign parameter. r: Region - Allocation region for the returned result. Returns ParseResult Notes args must not include the executable name. The parsed dictionaries are allocated in r . Options may be written with their long or short names. -- ends option parsing. Repeating an option is a parse error. Supported forms are --long and -s ; grouped short options and inline values such as --output=file are not supported. Option values are retrieved by name, such as result.options[\"--output\"]?[\"file\"]? . Unknown or repeated options, missing option values, and missing or extra positional arguments return an argparse: error."},{"title":"bytes: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#types","text":"Types ByteBuf Mutable growable byte buffer allocated in a Region . Bytes Immutable byte sequence allocated in a Region ."},{"title":"bytes: ByteBuf","heading":"ByteBuf","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#bytebuf","text":"ByteBuf Mutable growable byte buffer allocated in a Region ."},{"title":"bytes: Bytes","heading":"Bytes","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#bytes-2","text":"Bytes Immutable byte sequence allocated in a Region ."},{"title":"bytes: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#functions","text":"Functions buf_append(buf: ByteBuf, bytes: Bytes) -> void Append all bytes from an immutable byte sequence. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. bytes: Bytes - Bytes to append. --- buf_append_string(buf: ByteBuf, foreign text: String) -> void Append a string's exact bytes. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. foreign text: String - String to append. --- buf_clear(buf: ByteBuf) -> void Clear a byte buffer without reclaiming region memory. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. --- buf_from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> ByteBuf Create a byte buffer initialized from a string's exact bytes. Parameters foreign text: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns New ByteBuf . --- buf_get(buf: ByteBuf, index: int) -> char Get one byte from a byte buffer by index. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. index: int - Byte index. Returns Byte at index . --- buf_len(buf: ByteBuf) -> int Get the length of a byte buffer. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. Returns Number of bytes currently stored. --- buf_new(region: Region) -> ByteBuf Create an empty mutable byte buffer. This is also the default initializer used by bytes::ByteBuf{} and typed declarations without an initializer. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Empty ByteBuf . --- buf_push(buf: ByteBuf, byte: char) -> void Append one byte. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. byte: char - Byte to append. --- throws buf_push_int(buf: ByteBuf, byte: int) -> void Append one integer value after validating it is a byte. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. byte: int - Integer byte value. Throws If byte is outside 0..255 . --- equals(left: Bytes, right: Bytes) -> bool Return whether two byte sequences have identical contents. Parameters left: Bytes - Left byte sequence. right: Bytes - Right byte sequence. Returns true if lengths and contents are equal. --- from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a string's exact bytes into a byte sequence. Parameters foreign text: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied Bytes . --- get(bytes: Bytes, index: int) -> char Get one byte by index. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. index: int - Byte index. Returns Byte at index . --- len(bytes: Bytes) -> int Get the length of a byte sequence. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. Returns Number of bytes. --- new(region: Region) -> Bytes Create an empty byte sequence. This is also the default initializer used by bytes::Bytes{} and typed declarations without an initializer. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Empty Bytes . --- slice(bytes: Bytes, start: int, stop: int, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a byte range. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. start: int - Start byte index, inclusive. stop: int - Stop byte index, exclusive. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied byte range. --- to_bytes(buf: ByteBuf, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a byte buffer into an immutable byte sequence. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied Bytes . --- to_hex(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String Convert bytes to lowercase hexadecimal text. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Hexadecimal representation. --- throws to_string_ascii(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String Convert known ASCII text bytes to a String . Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied ASCII text. Throws If any byte is 0 or greater than 127 ."},{"title":"bytes: buf_append(buf: ByteBuf, bytes: Bytes) -> void","heading":"buf_append(buf: ByteBuf, bytes: Bytes) -> void","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-append-buf-bytebuf-bytes-bytes-void","text":"buf_append(buf: ByteBuf, bytes: Bytes) -> void Append all bytes from an immutable byte sequence. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. bytes: Bytes - Bytes to append. ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_append_string(buf: ByteBuf, foreign text: String) -> void","heading":"buf_append_string(buf: ByteBuf, foreign text: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-append-string-buf-bytebuf-foreign-text-string-void","text":"buf_append_string(buf: ByteBuf, foreign text: String) -> void Append a string's exact bytes. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. foreign text: String - String to append. ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_clear(buf: ByteBuf) -> void","heading":"buf_clear(buf: ByteBuf) -> void","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-clear-buf-bytebuf-void","text":"buf_clear(buf: ByteBuf) -> void Clear a byte buffer without reclaiming region memory. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> ByteBuf","heading":"buf_from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> ByteBuf","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-from-string-foreign-text-string-region-region-bytebuf","text":"buf_from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> ByteBuf Create a byte buffer initialized from a string's exact bytes. Parameters foreign text: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns New ByteBuf . ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_get(buf: ByteBuf, index: int) -> char","heading":"buf_get(buf: ByteBuf, index: int) -> char","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-get-buf-bytebuf-index-int-char","text":"buf_get(buf: ByteBuf, index: int) -> char Get one byte from a byte buffer by index. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. index: int - Byte index. Returns Byte at index . ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_len(buf: ByteBuf) -> int","heading":"buf_len(buf: ByteBuf) -> int","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-len-buf-bytebuf-int","text":"buf_len(buf: ByteBuf) -> int Get the length of a byte buffer. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. Returns Number of bytes currently stored. ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_new(region: Region) -> ByteBuf","heading":"buf_new(region: Region) -> ByteBuf","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-new-region-region-bytebuf","text":"buf_new(region: Region) -> ByteBuf Create an empty mutable byte buffer. This is also the default initializer used by bytes::ByteBuf{} and typed declarations without an initializer. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Empty ByteBuf . ---"},{"title":"bytes: buf_push(buf: ByteBuf, byte: char) -> void","heading":"buf_push(buf: ByteBuf, byte: char) -> void","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#buf-push-buf-bytebuf-byte-char-void","text":"buf_push(buf: ByteBuf, byte: char) -> void Append one byte. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. byte: char - Byte to append. ---"},{"title":"bytes: throws buf_push_int(buf: ByteBuf, byte: int) -> void","heading":"throws buf_push_int(buf: ByteBuf, byte: int) -> void","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#throws-buf-push-int-buf-bytebuf-byte-int-void","text":"throws buf_push_int(buf: ByteBuf, byte: int) -> void Append one integer value after validating it is a byte. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. byte: int - Integer byte value. Throws If byte is outside 0..255 . ---"},{"title":"bytes: equals(left: Bytes, right: Bytes) -> bool","heading":"equals(left: Bytes, right: Bytes) -> bool","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#equals-left-bytes-right-bytes-bool","text":"equals(left: Bytes, right: Bytes) -> bool Return whether two byte sequences have identical contents. Parameters left: Bytes - Left byte sequence. right: Bytes - Right byte sequence. Returns true if lengths and contents are equal. ---"},{"title":"bytes: from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> Bytes","heading":"from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> Bytes","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#from-string-foreign-text-string-region-region-bytes","text":"from_string(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a string's exact bytes into a byte sequence. Parameters foreign text: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied Bytes . ---"},{"title":"bytes: get(bytes: Bytes, index: int) -> char","heading":"get(bytes: Bytes, index: int) -> char","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#get-bytes-bytes-index-int-char","text":"get(bytes: Bytes, index: int) -> char Get one byte by index. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. index: int - Byte index. Returns Byte at index . ---"},{"title":"bytes: len(bytes: Bytes) -> int","heading":"len(bytes: Bytes) -> int","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#len-bytes-bytes-int","text":"len(bytes: Bytes) -> int Get the length of a byte sequence. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. Returns Number of bytes. ---"},{"title":"bytes: new(region: Region) -> Bytes","heading":"new(region: Region) -> Bytes","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#new-region-region-bytes","text":"new(region: Region) -> Bytes Create an empty byte sequence. This is also the default initializer used by bytes::Bytes{} and typed declarations without an initializer. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Empty Bytes . ---"},{"title":"bytes: slice(bytes: Bytes, start: int, stop: int, region: Region) -> Bytes","heading":"slice(bytes: Bytes, start: int, stop: int, region: Region) -> Bytes","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#slice-bytes-bytes-start-int-stop-int-region-region-bytes","text":"slice(bytes: Bytes, start: int, stop: int, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a byte range. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. start: int - Start byte index, inclusive. stop: int - Stop byte index, exclusive. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied byte range. ---"},{"title":"bytes: to_bytes(buf: ByteBuf, region: Region) -> Bytes","heading":"to_bytes(buf: ByteBuf, region: Region) -> Bytes","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#to-bytes-buf-bytebuf-region-region-bytes","text":"to_bytes(buf: ByteBuf, region: Region) -> Bytes Copy a byte buffer into an immutable byte sequence. Parameters buf: ByteBuf - Byte buffer. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied Bytes . ---"},{"title":"bytes: to_hex(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String","heading":"to_hex(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#to-hex-bytes-bytes-region-region-string","text":"to_hex(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String Convert bytes to lowercase hexadecimal text. Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Hexadecimal representation. ---"},{"title":"bytes: throws to_string_ascii(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String","heading":"throws to_string_ascii(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/bytes.html#throws-to-string-ascii-bytes-bytes-region-region-string","text":"throws to_string_ascii(bytes: Bytes, region: Region) -> String Convert known ASCII text bytes to a String . Parameters bytes: Bytes - Byte sequence. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns Copied ASCII text. Throws If any byte is 0 or greater than 127 ."},{"title":"char: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/char.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"char: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/char.html#functions","text":"Functions isalnum(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isalnum. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an alphabet letter or digit, false otherwise. --- isalpha(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isalpha. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an alphabet letter, false otherwise. --- isdigit(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isdigit. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a digit, false otherwise. --- islower(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around islower. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a lowercase alphabet letter, false otherwise. --- isspace(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isspace. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a whitespace character, false otherwise. --- isupper(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isupper. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an uppercase alphabet letter, false otherwise. --- isxdigit(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isxdigit. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a hexadecimal digit, false otherwise. --- tolower(ch: char) -> char Wrapper around tolower. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns Lowercase variant if possible, otherwise returns input. --- toupper(ch: char) -> char Wrapper around toupper. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns Uppercase variant if possible, otherwise returns input."},{"title":"char: isalnum(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isalnum(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isalnum-ch-char-bool","text":"isalnum(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isalnum. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an alphabet letter or digit, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: isalpha(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isalpha(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isalpha-ch-char-bool","text":"isalpha(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isalpha. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an alphabet letter, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: isdigit(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isdigit(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isdigit-ch-char-bool","text":"isdigit(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isdigit. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a digit, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: islower(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"islower(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#islower-ch-char-bool","text":"islower(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around islower. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a lowercase alphabet letter, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: isspace(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isspace(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isspace-ch-char-bool","text":"isspace(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isspace. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a whitespace character, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: isupper(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isupper(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isupper-ch-char-bool","text":"isupper(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isupper. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is an uppercase alphabet letter, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: isxdigit(ch: char) -> bool","heading":"isxdigit(ch: char) -> bool","url":"jiklib/char.html#isxdigit-ch-char-bool","text":"isxdigit(ch: char) -> bool Wrapper around isxdigit. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns true if character is a hexadecimal digit, false otherwise. ---"},{"title":"char: tolower(ch: char) -> char","heading":"tolower(ch: char) -> char","url":"jiklib/char.html#tolower-ch-char-char","text":"tolower(ch: char) -> char Wrapper around tolower. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns Lowercase variant if possible, otherwise returns input. ---"},{"title":"char: toupper(ch: char) -> char","heading":"toupper(ch: char) -> char","url":"jiklib/char.html#toupper-ch-char-char","text":"toupper(ch: char) -> char Wrapper around toupper. Parameters ch: char - Character. Returns Uppercase variant if possible, otherwise returns input."},{"title":"fs: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/fs.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"fs: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/fs.html#functions","text":"Functions exists(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path exists. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true if the path exists, otherwise false . --- is_file(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path refers to a regular file. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true only for regular files. --- is_dir(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path refers to a directory. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true only for directories. --- throws mkdir(path: String) -> void Create a directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. --- throws mkdir_all(path: String) -> void Create a directory path and any missing parents. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. --- throws remove_file(path: String) -> void Remove a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - File path. --- throws remove_dir(path: String) -> void Remove an empty directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. --- throws remove_dir_all(path: String) -> void Remove a directory tree recursively. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. Notes Implemented in Jik by composing read_dir , predicates, and removal primitives. Symlink-specific behavior is not guaranteed in v1. --- throws rename(foreign old_path: String, foreign new_path: String) -> void Rename or move a filesystem object. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign old_path: String - Source path. foreign new_path: String - Destination path. --- throws copy_file(foreign src_path: String, foreign dst_path: String) -> void Copy a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign src_path: String - Source path. foreign dst_path: String - Destination path. --- throws read_dir(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> Vec[String] Return the names of entries directly inside a directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - Directory path. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned vector and strings. Returns Directory entry names. Notes Entry names are returned without directory prefixes. Entry ordering is platform-dependent. --- temp_dir(region: Region) -> String Return the platform temporary directory path. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Temporary directory path."},{"title":"fs: exists(path: String) -> bool","heading":"exists(path: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/fs.html#exists-path-string-bool","text":"exists(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path exists. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true if the path exists, otherwise false . ---"},{"title":"fs: is_file(path: String) -> bool","heading":"is_file(path: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/fs.html#is-file-path-string-bool","text":"is_file(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path refers to a regular file. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true only for regular files. ---"},{"title":"fs: is_dir(path: String) -> bool","heading":"is_dir(path: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/fs.html#is-dir-path-string-bool","text":"is_dir(path: String) -> bool Return whether a filesystem path refers to a directory. Parameters path: String - Filesystem path. Returns true only for directories. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws mkdir(path: String) -> void","heading":"throws mkdir(path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-mkdir-path-string-void","text":"throws mkdir(path: String) -> void Create a directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws mkdir_all(path: String) -> void","heading":"throws mkdir_all(path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-mkdir-all-path-string-void","text":"throws mkdir_all(path: String) -> void Create a directory path and any missing parents. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws remove_file(path: String) -> void","heading":"throws remove_file(path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-remove-file-path-string-void","text":"throws remove_file(path: String) -> void Remove a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - File path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws remove_dir(path: String) -> void","heading":"throws remove_dir(path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-remove-dir-path-string-void","text":"throws remove_dir(path: String) -> void Remove an empty directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws remove_dir_all(path: String) -> void","heading":"throws remove_dir_all(path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-remove-dir-all-path-string-void","text":"throws remove_dir_all(path: String) -> void Remove a directory tree recursively. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters path: String - Directory path. Notes Implemented in Jik by composing read_dir , predicates, and removal primitives. Symlink-specific behavior is not guaranteed in v1. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws rename(foreign old_path: String, foreign new_path: String) -> void","heading":"throws rename(foreign old_path: String, foreign new_path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-rename-foreign-old-path-string-foreign-new-path-string-void","text":"throws rename(foreign old_path: String, foreign new_path: String) -> void Rename or move a filesystem object. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign old_path: String - Source path. foreign new_path: String - Destination path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws copy_file(foreign src_path: String, foreign dst_path: String) -> void","heading":"throws copy_file(foreign src_path: String, foreign dst_path: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-copy-file-foreign-src-path-string-foreign-dst-path-string-void","text":"throws copy_file(foreign src_path: String, foreign dst_path: String) -> void Copy a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign src_path: String - Source path. foreign dst_path: String - Destination path. ---"},{"title":"fs: throws read_dir(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> Vec[String]","heading":"throws read_dir(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> Vec[String]","url":"jiklib/fs.html#throws-read-dir-foreign-path-string-region-region-vec-string","text":"throws read_dir(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> Vec[String] Return the names of entries directly inside a directory. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - Directory path. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned vector and strings. Returns Directory entry names. Notes Entry names are returned without directory prefixes. Entry ordering is platform-dependent. ---"},{"title":"fs: temp_dir(region: Region) -> String","heading":"temp_dir(region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/fs.html#temp-dir-region-region-string","text":"temp_dir(region: Region) -> String Return the platform temporary directory path. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Temporary directory path."},{"title":"io: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/io.html#types","text":"Types File Opaque file handle."},{"title":"io: File","heading":"File","url":"jiklib/io.html#file","text":"File Opaque file handle."},{"title":"io: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/io.html#functions","text":"Functions throws close(file: File) -> void Close an open file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters file: File - File handle. --- throws open(foreign path: String, foreign mode: String, region: Region) -> File Open a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. foreign mode: String - File open mode. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state. Returns Open file handle. Notes Supported modes are \"r\" (read), \"w\" (write), and \"a\" (append). --- throws read(foreign file: File, region: Region) -> String Read the full contents of a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign file: File - File handle. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Full file contents. Notes The file must have been opened in read mode. --- throws read_file(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> String Read an entire file by path. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state and the returned string. Returns Full file contents. --- throws write(file: File, foreign text: String) -> void Write a string to a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters file: File - File handle. foreign text: String - String to write. Notes The file must have been opened in write or append mode. --- throws write_file(foreign path: String, foreign text: String, region: Region) -> void Write a string to a file by path, truncating existing contents. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. foreign text: String - String to write. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state. ---"},{"title":"io: throws close(file: File) -> void","heading":"throws close(file: File) -> void","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-close-file-file-void","text":"throws close(file: File) -> void Close an open file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters file: File - File handle. ---"},{"title":"io: throws open(foreign path: String, foreign mode: String, region: Region) -> File","heading":"throws open(foreign path: String, foreign mode: String, region: Region) -> File","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-open-foreign-path-string-foreign-mode-string-region-region-file","text":"throws open(foreign path: String, foreign mode: String, region: Region) -> File Open a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. foreign mode: String - File open mode. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state. Returns Open file handle. Notes Supported modes are \"r\" (read), \"w\" (write), and \"a\" (append). ---"},{"title":"io: throws read(foreign file: File, region: Region) -> String","heading":"throws read(foreign file: File, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-read-foreign-file-file-region-region-string","text":"throws read(foreign file: File, region: Region) -> String Read the full contents of a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign file: File - File handle. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Full file contents. Notes The file must have been opened in read mode. ---"},{"title":"io: throws read_file(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> String","heading":"throws read_file(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-read-file-foreign-path-string-region-region-string","text":"throws read_file(foreign path: String, region: Region) -> String Read an entire file by path. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state and the returned string. Returns Full file contents. ---"},{"title":"io: throws write(file: File, foreign text: String) -> void","heading":"throws write(file: File, foreign text: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-write-file-file-foreign-text-string-void","text":"throws write(file: File, foreign text: String) -> void Write a string to a file. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters file: File - File handle. foreign text: String - String to write. Notes The file must have been opened in write or append mode. ---"},{"title":"io: throws write_file(foreign path: String, foreign text: String, region: Region) -> void","heading":"throws write_file(foreign path: String, foreign text: String, region: Region) -> void","url":"jiklib/io.html#throws-write-file-foreign-path-string-foreign-text-string-region-region-void","text":"throws write_file(foreign path: String, foreign text: String, region: Region) -> void Write a string to a file by path, truncating existing contents. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign path: String - File path. foreign text: String - String to write. region: Region - Allocation region for file handle state. ---"},{"title":"math: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/math.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"math: Constants","heading":"Constants","url":"jiklib/math.html#constants","text":"Constants PI pi. --- E Euler's number e. ---"},{"title":"math: PI","heading":"PI","url":"jiklib/math.html#pi","text":"PI pi. ---"},{"title":"math: E","heading":"E","url":"jiklib/math.html#e","text":"E Euler's number e. ---"},{"title":"math: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/math.html#functions","text":"Functions abs(x: double) -> double Compute the absolute value. Parameters x: double - Input value. Returns Absolute value of x . --- approx_eq(a: double, b: double, eps: double) -> bool Check whether two values are equal within a tolerance. Parameters a: double - Left-hand value. b: double - Right-hand value. eps: double - Maximum allowed absolute difference. Returns True if values are within tolerance, else false. Notes Returns true if abs(a - b) <= eps . --- atan2(y: double, x: double) -> double Compute the arctangent of y/x using the signs of y and x . Parameters y: double - y coordinate. x: double - x coordinate. Returns Angle in radians. Notes The result is in radians. --- cos(x: double) -> double Compute the cosine of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Cosine of x . --- tan(x: double) -> double Compute the tangent of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Tangent of x . --- floor(x: double) -> double Round down to the next integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Largest integer value not greater than x . --- ceil(x: double) -> double Round up to the next integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Smallest integer value not less than x . --- round(x: double) -> double Round to the nearest integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Nearest integer value to x . Notes Halfway cases follow the platform C math library. --- pow(x: double, y: double) -> double Raise a value to a power. Parameters x: double - Base value. y: double - Exponent. Returns x raised to the power y . --- log(x: double) -> double Compute the natural logarithm. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Natural logarithm of x . --- exp(x: double) -> double Compute the natural exponential. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns e raised to the power x . --- fmod(x: double, y: double) -> double Compute the floating-point remainder (C fmod ). Parameters x: double - Numerator. y: double - Denominator. Returns Remainder. --- is_finite(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is finite. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if finite (not NaN and not +/-Inf), else 0. --- is_inf(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is positive or negative infinity. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if x is +/-Inf, else 0. --- is_nan(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is NaN. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if NaN, else 0. --- max(a: double, b: double) -> double Return the larger of two values. Parameters a: double - First value to compare. b: double - Second value to compare. Returns The larger value. --- min(a: double, b: double) -> double Return the smaller of two values. Parameters a: double - First value to compare. b: double - Second value to compare. Returns The smaller value. --- sin(x: double) -> double Compute the sine of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Sine of x . --- sqrt(x: double) -> double Compute the square root. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Square root of x ."},{"title":"math: abs(x: double) -> double","heading":"abs(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#abs-x-double-double","text":"abs(x: double) -> double Compute the absolute value. Parameters x: double - Input value. Returns Absolute value of x . ---"},{"title":"math: approx_eq(a: double, b: double, eps: double) -> bool","heading":"approx_eq(a: double, b: double, eps: double) -> bool","url":"jiklib/math.html#approx-eq-a-double-b-double-eps-double-bool","text":"approx_eq(a: double, b: double, eps: double) -> bool Check whether two values are equal within a tolerance. Parameters a: double - Left-hand value. b: double - Right-hand value. eps: double - Maximum allowed absolute difference. Returns True if values are within tolerance, else false. Notes Returns true if abs(a - b) <= eps . ---"},{"title":"math: atan2(y: double, x: double) -> double","heading":"atan2(y: double, x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#atan2-y-double-x-double-double","text":"atan2(y: double, x: double) -> double Compute the arctangent of y/x using the signs of y and x . Parameters y: double - y coordinate. x: double - x coordinate. Returns Angle in radians. Notes The result is in radians. ---"},{"title":"math: cos(x: double) -> double","heading":"cos(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#cos-x-double-double","text":"cos(x: double) -> double Compute the cosine of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Cosine of x . ---"},{"title":"math: tan(x: double) -> double","heading":"tan(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#tan-x-double-double","text":"tan(x: double) -> double Compute the tangent of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Tangent of x . ---"},{"title":"math: floor(x: double) -> double","heading":"floor(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#floor-x-double-double","text":"floor(x: double) -> double Round down to the next integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Largest integer value not greater than x . ---"},{"title":"math: ceil(x: double) -> double","heading":"ceil(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#ceil-x-double-double","text":"ceil(x: double) -> double Round up to the next integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Smallest integer value not less than x . ---"},{"title":"math: round(x: double) -> double","heading":"round(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#round-x-double-double","text":"round(x: double) -> double Round to the nearest integer value. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Nearest integer value to x . Notes Halfway cases follow the platform C math library. ---"},{"title":"math: pow(x: double, y: double) -> double","heading":"pow(x: double, y: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#pow-x-double-y-double-double","text":"pow(x: double, y: double) -> double Raise a value to a power. Parameters x: double - Base value. y: double - Exponent. Returns x raised to the power y . ---"},{"title":"math: log(x: double) -> double","heading":"log(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#log-x-double-double","text":"log(x: double) -> double Compute the natural logarithm. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Natural logarithm of x . ---"},{"title":"math: exp(x: double) -> double","heading":"exp(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#exp-x-double-double","text":"exp(x: double) -> double Compute the natural exponential. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns e raised to the power x . ---"},{"title":"math: fmod(x: double, y: double) -> double","heading":"fmod(x: double, y: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#fmod-x-double-y-double-double","text":"fmod(x: double, y: double) -> double Compute the floating-point remainder (C fmod ). Parameters x: double - Numerator. y: double - Denominator. Returns Remainder. ---"},{"title":"math: is_finite(x: double) -> int","heading":"is_finite(x: double) -> int","url":"jiklib/math.html#is-finite-x-double-int","text":"is_finite(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is finite. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if finite (not NaN and not +/-Inf), else 0. ---"},{"title":"math: is_inf(x: double) -> int","heading":"is_inf(x: double) -> int","url":"jiklib/math.html#is-inf-x-double-int","text":"is_inf(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is positive or negative infinity. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if x is +/-Inf, else 0. ---"},{"title":"math: is_nan(x: double) -> int","heading":"is_nan(x: double) -> int","url":"jiklib/math.html#is-nan-x-double-int","text":"is_nan(x: double) -> int Test whether the value is NaN. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns 1 if NaN, else 0. ---"},{"title":"math: max(a: double, b: double) -> double","heading":"max(a: double, b: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#max-a-double-b-double-double","text":"max(a: double, b: double) -> double Return the larger of two values. Parameters a: double - First value to compare. b: double - Second value to compare. Returns The larger value. ---"},{"title":"math: min(a: double, b: double) -> double","heading":"min(a: double, b: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#min-a-double-b-double-double","text":"min(a: double, b: double) -> double Return the smaller of two values. Parameters a: double - First value to compare. b: double - Second value to compare. Returns The smaller value. ---"},{"title":"math: sin(x: double) -> double","heading":"sin(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#sin-x-double-double","text":"sin(x: double) -> double Compute the sine of an angle in radians. Parameters x: double - Angle in radians. Returns Sine of x . ---"},{"title":"math: sqrt(x: double) -> double","heading":"sqrt(x: double) -> double","url":"jiklib/math.html#sqrt-x-double-double","text":"sqrt(x: double) -> double Compute the square root. Parameters x: double - Value. Returns Square root of x ."},{"title":"path: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/path.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"path: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/path.html#functions","text":"Functions join(foreign parts: Vec[String], r: Region) -> String Join path segments using the platform separator. Parameters foreign parts: Vec[String] - Path segments to join. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Combined path string. Notes Empty segments are ignored. --- normalize(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Normalize a path lexically. Parameters path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Lexically normalized path. Notes Repeated separators and . components are removed. .. components remove the preceding normal component when possible. Unresolved .. components are preserved in relative paths and discarded at an absolute root. This function does not access the filesystem, resolve symlinks, expand home or environment references, or support Windows UNC and device paths. --- basename(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the last path component. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Last path component, or an empty string for root-like inputs. --- dirname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the parent directory. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Parent directory path. Notes Root-like inputs are kept stable. A path with no directory component returns \".\" . --- extname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the extension of the last path component. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Extension text including the leading dot, or \"\" when no extension exists. --- is_absolute(path: String) -> bool Return whether path is absolute. Parameters path: String - Input path. Returns true if the path is absolute, otherwise false . Notes Windows paths accept both \\ and / separators. Drive-root forms such as C:\\foo are treated as absolute."},{"title":"path: join(foreign parts: Vec[String], r: Region) -> String","heading":"join(foreign parts: Vec[String], r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/path.html#join-foreign-parts-vec-string-r-region-string","text":"join(foreign parts: Vec[String], r: Region) -> String Join path segments using the platform separator. Parameters foreign parts: Vec[String] - Path segments to join. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Combined path string. Notes Empty segments are ignored. ---"},{"title":"path: normalize(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","heading":"normalize(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/path.html#normalize-foreign-path-string-r-region-string","text":"normalize(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Normalize a path lexically. Parameters path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Lexically normalized path. Notes Repeated separators and . components are removed. .. components remove the preceding normal component when possible. Unresolved .. components are preserved in relative paths and discarded at an absolute root. This function does not access the filesystem, resolve symlinks, expand home or environment references, or support Windows UNC and device paths. ---"},{"title":"path: basename(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","heading":"basename(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/path.html#basename-foreign-path-string-r-region-string","text":"basename(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the last path component. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Last path component, or an empty string for root-like inputs. ---"},{"title":"path: dirname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","heading":"dirname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/path.html#dirname-foreign-path-string-r-region-string","text":"dirname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the parent directory. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Parent directory path. Notes Root-like inputs are kept stable. A path with no directory component returns \".\" . ---"},{"title":"path: extname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","heading":"extname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/path.html#extname-foreign-path-string-r-region-string","text":"extname(foreign path: String, r: Region) -> String Return the extension of the last path component. Parameters foreign path: String - Input path. r: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Extension text including the leading dot, or \"\" when no extension exists. ---"},{"title":"path: is_absolute(path: String) -> bool","heading":"is_absolute(path: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/path.html#is-absolute-path-string-bool","text":"is_absolute(path: String) -> bool Return whether path is absolute. Parameters path: String - Input path. Returns true if the path is absolute, otherwise false . Notes Windows paths accept both \\ and / separators. Drive-root forms such as C:\\foo are treated as absolute."},{"title":"process: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/process.html#types","text":"Types Result Completed captured child process result. Fields: code: int - Child exit code. out: bytes::Bytes - Captured stdout bytes. err: bytes::Bytes - Captured stderr bytes."},{"title":"process: Result","heading":"Result","url":"jiklib/process.html#result","text":"Result Completed captured child process result. Fields: code: int - Child exit code. out: bytes::Bytes - Captured stdout bytes. err: bytes::Bytes - Captured stderr bytes."},{"title":"process: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/process.html#functions","text":"Functions throws run(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String]) -> int Run a child process with inherited stdio and wait for it. Behavior Throws if the child cannot be started or if waiting for it fails. A nonzero child exit code is returned, not thrown. No shell is invoked. Parameters foreign program: String - Executable name or path. foreign args: Vec[String] - Child process arguments. Returns Child exit code. Notes args contains only child arguments, not argv[0] . --- throws capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result Run a child process, capture stdout and stderr, and wait for it. Behavior Throws if pipes cannot be created, the child cannot be started, waiting fails, or pipe reads fail. A nonzero child exit code is returned in Result.code , not thrown. No shell is invoked. Stdout and stderr are captured separately as raw bytes. Stdin is inherited from the parent process. Parameters foreign program: String - Executable name or path. foreign args: Vec[String] - Child process arguments. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned result and captured bytes. Returns Result containing the child exit code, stdout bytes, and stderr bytes. Notes args contains only child arguments, not argv[0] . Captured output may not be valid text. Use bytes helpers when decoding or inspecting it."},{"title":"process: throws run(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String]) -> int","heading":"throws run(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String]) -> int","url":"jiklib/process.html#throws-run-foreign-program-string-foreign-args-vec-string-int","text":"throws run(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String]) -> int Run a child process with inherited stdio and wait for it. Behavior Throws if the child cannot be started or if waiting for it fails. A nonzero child exit code is returned, not thrown. No shell is invoked. Parameters foreign program: String - Executable name or path. foreign args: Vec[String] - Child process arguments. Returns Child exit code. Notes args contains only child arguments, not argv[0] . ---"},{"title":"process: throws capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result","heading":"throws capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result","url":"jiklib/process.html#throws-capture-foreign-program-string-foreign-args-vec-string-region-region-result","text":"throws capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result Run a child process, capture stdout and stderr, and wait for it. Behavior Throws if pipes cannot be created, the child cannot be started, waiting fails, or pipe reads fail. A nonzero child exit code is returned in Result.code , not thrown. No shell is invoked. Stdout and stderr are captured separately as raw bytes. Stdin is inherited from the parent process. Parameters foreign program: String - Executable name or path. foreign args: Vec[String] - Child process arguments. region: Region - Allocation region for the returned result and captured bytes. Returns Result containing the child exit code, stdout bytes, and stderr bytes. Notes args contains only child arguments, not argv[0] . Captured output may not be valid text. Use bytes helpers when decoding or inspecting it."},{"title":"rand: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/rand.html#types","text":"Types Rng Opaque random number generator state."},{"title":"rand: Rng","heading":"Rng","url":"jiklib/rand.html#rng","text":"Rng Opaque random number generator state."},{"title":"rand: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/rand.html#functions","text":"Functions new(seed: int, region: Region) -> Rng Create a new RNG with a deterministic seed. Parameters seed: int - Seed value. region: Region - Allocation region for RNG state. Returns A new RNG. --- new_time(region: Region) -> Rng Create a new RNG seeded from the current time. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for RNG state. Returns A new RNG. --- next_double(rng: Rng) -> double Generate a random floating-point number. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. Returns Uniform double in [0.0, 1.0). --- next_int(rng: Rng) -> int Generate a non-negative random integer. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. Returns Uniform int in [0, 2^31 - 1]. --- throws range_int(rng: Rng, lo: int, hi: int) -> int Generate a random integer in a half-open interval. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. lo: int - Lower bound (inclusive). hi: int - Upper bound (exclusive). Returns Uniform int in [lo, hi)."},{"title":"rand: new(seed: int, region: Region) -> Rng","heading":"new(seed: int, region: Region) -> Rng","url":"jiklib/rand.html#new-seed-int-region-region-rng","text":"new(seed: int, region: Region) -> Rng Create a new RNG with a deterministic seed. Parameters seed: int - Seed value. region: Region - Allocation region for RNG state. Returns A new RNG. ---"},{"title":"rand: new_time(region: Region) -> Rng","heading":"new_time(region: Region) -> Rng","url":"jiklib/rand.html#new-time-region-region-rng","text":"new_time(region: Region) -> Rng Create a new RNG seeded from the current time. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for RNG state. Returns A new RNG. ---"},{"title":"rand: next_double(rng: Rng) -> double","heading":"next_double(rng: Rng) -> double","url":"jiklib/rand.html#next-double-rng-rng-double","text":"next_double(rng: Rng) -> double Generate a random floating-point number. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. Returns Uniform double in [0.0, 1.0). ---"},{"title":"rand: next_int(rng: Rng) -> int","heading":"next_int(rng: Rng) -> int","url":"jiklib/rand.html#next-int-rng-rng-int","text":"next_int(rng: Rng) -> int Generate a non-negative random integer. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. Returns Uniform int in [0, 2^31 - 1]. ---"},{"title":"rand: throws range_int(rng: Rng, lo: int, hi: int) -> int","heading":"throws range_int(rng: Rng, lo: int, hi: int) -> int","url":"jiklib/rand.html#throws-range-int-rng-rng-lo-int-hi-int-int","text":"throws range_int(rng: Rng, lo: int, hi: int) -> int Generate a random integer in a half-open interval. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters rng: Rng - RNG. lo: int - Lower bound (inclusive). hi: int - Upper bound (exclusive). Returns Uniform int in [lo, hi)."},{"title":"region: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/region.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"region: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/region.html#functions","text":"Functions used_bytes(region: Region) -> int Return the number of allocator-consumed bytes in the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Total bytes currently marked as used across all region blocks. Notes This is allocator-level usage, not exact live object size. It includes allocator padding and dead bytes left behind by region growth. --- total_bytes(region: Region) -> int Return the total capacity of all blocks currently owned by the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Total block capacity in bytes. --- block_count(region: Region) -> int Return the number of allocation blocks currently owned by the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Number of blocks in the region. --- print_stats(region: Region) -> void Print a short region allocator summary to standard output. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Notes The printed summary includes the region address together with used bytes, total bytes, and block count."},{"title":"region: used_bytes(region: Region) -> int","heading":"used_bytes(region: Region) -> int","url":"jiklib/region.html#used-bytes-region-region-int","text":"used_bytes(region: Region) -> int Return the number of allocator-consumed bytes in the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Total bytes currently marked as used across all region blocks. Notes This is allocator-level usage, not exact live object size. It includes allocator padding and dead bytes left behind by region growth. ---"},{"title":"region: total_bytes(region: Region) -> int","heading":"total_bytes(region: Region) -> int","url":"jiklib/region.html#total-bytes-region-region-int","text":"total_bytes(region: Region) -> int Return the total capacity of all blocks currently owned by the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Total block capacity in bytes. ---"},{"title":"region: block_count(region: Region) -> int","heading":"block_count(region: Region) -> int","url":"jiklib/region.html#block-count-region-region-int","text":"block_count(region: Region) -> int Return the number of allocation blocks currently owned by the region. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Returns Number of blocks in the region. ---"},{"title":"region: print_stats(region: Region) -> void","heading":"print_stats(region: Region) -> void","url":"jiklib/region.html#print-stats-region-region-void","text":"print_stats(region: Region) -> void Print a short region allocator summary to standard output. Parameters region: Region - Region to inspect. Notes The printed summary includes the region address together with used bytes, total bytes, and block count."},{"title":"std: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/std.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"std: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/std.html#functions","text":"Functions input(region: Region) -> String Read a line of input from standard input. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Input line as a String. --- panic(msg: String) -> void Terminate the program with an error message. Parameters msg: String - Message."},{"title":"std: input(region: Region) -> String","heading":"input(region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/std.html#input-region-region-string","text":"input(region: Region) -> String Read a line of input from standard input. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Input line as a String. ---"},{"title":"std: panic(msg: String) -> void","heading":"panic(msg: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/std.html#panic-msg-string-void","text":"panic(msg: String) -> void Terminate the program with an error message. Parameters msg: String - Message."},{"title":"strbuf: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#types","text":"Types StrBuf Opaque string buffer allocated in a Region ."},{"title":"strbuf: StrBuf","heading":"StrBuf","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#strbuf-2","text":"StrBuf Opaque string buffer allocated in a Region ."},{"title":"strbuf: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#functions","text":"Functions append(buf: StrBuf, foreign text: String) -> void Append a string to the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. foreign text: String - String to append. --- append_char(buf: StrBuf, ch: char) -> void Append a single byte to the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. ch: char - Byte to append. --- clear(buf: StrBuf) -> void Clears the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Notes Does not deallocate or free anything, just resets the buffer pointer. --- len(buf: StrBuf) -> int Get the length of the buffer in bytes. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Returns The number of bytes in the buffer. --- new(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> StrBuf Create a new buffer from initial content. Parameters foreign text: String - Initial content. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns A new StrBuf . --- pop(buf: StrBuf) -> char Pop a single byte from the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Returns A byte from the end of the buffer. --- print(buf: StrBuf) -> void Prints the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. --- to_string(foreign buf: StrBuf, region: Region) -> String Converts the buffer contents to a String. Parameters foreign buf: StrBuf - Buffer. region: Region - Region to allocate the String. Returns Contents of the buffer as a String."},{"title":"strbuf: append(buf: StrBuf, foreign text: String) -> void","heading":"append(buf: StrBuf, foreign text: String) -> void","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#append-buf-strbuf-foreign-text-string-void","text":"append(buf: StrBuf, foreign text: String) -> void Append a string to the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. foreign text: String - String to append. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: append_char(buf: StrBuf, ch: char) -> void","heading":"append_char(buf: StrBuf, ch: char) -> void","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#append-char-buf-strbuf-ch-char-void","text":"append_char(buf: StrBuf, ch: char) -> void Append a single byte to the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. ch: char - Byte to append. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: clear(buf: StrBuf) -> void","heading":"clear(buf: StrBuf) -> void","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#clear-buf-strbuf-void","text":"clear(buf: StrBuf) -> void Clears the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Notes Does not deallocate or free anything, just resets the buffer pointer. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: len(buf: StrBuf) -> int","heading":"len(buf: StrBuf) -> int","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#len-buf-strbuf-int","text":"len(buf: StrBuf) -> int Get the length of the buffer in bytes. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Returns The number of bytes in the buffer. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: new(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> StrBuf","heading":"new(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> StrBuf","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#new-foreign-text-string-region-region-strbuf","text":"new(foreign text: String, region: Region) -> StrBuf Create a new buffer from initial content. Parameters foreign text: String - Initial content. region: Region - Allocation region. Returns A new StrBuf . ---"},{"title":"strbuf: pop(buf: StrBuf) -> char","heading":"pop(buf: StrBuf) -> char","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#pop-buf-strbuf-char","text":"pop(buf: StrBuf) -> char Pop a single byte from the end of the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. Returns A byte from the end of the buffer. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: print(buf: StrBuf) -> void","heading":"print(buf: StrBuf) -> void","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#print-buf-strbuf-void","text":"print(buf: StrBuf) -> void Prints the buffer. Parameters buf: StrBuf - Buffer. ---"},{"title":"strbuf: to_string(foreign buf: StrBuf, region: Region) -> String","heading":"to_string(foreign buf: StrBuf, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/strbuf.html#to-string-foreign-buf-strbuf-region-region-string","text":"to_string(foreign buf: StrBuf, region: Region) -> String Converts the buffer contents to a String. Parameters foreign buf: StrBuf - Buffer. region: Region - Region to allocate the String. Returns Contents of the buffer as a String."},{"title":"string: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/string.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"string: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/string.html#functions","text":"Functions from_int(value: int, region: Region) -> String Convert an integer to a string. Parameters value: int - Integer value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated decimal string. --- from_double(value: double, region: Region) -> String Convert a double to a string. Parameters value: double - Double value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string representation of the double. --- from_bool(value: bool, region: Region) -> String Convert a boolean to a string. Parameters value: bool - Boolean value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns \"true\" or \"false\" . --- from_char(value: char, region: Region) -> String Convert a character to a string. Parameters value: char - Character value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated one-byte string. --- concat(foreign left: String, foreign right: String, region: Region) -> String Concatenate two strings. Parameters foreign left: String - Left string. foreign right: String - Right string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Concatenated string. --- compare(foreign left: String, foreign right: String) -> int Compare two strings lexicographically by byte value. Parameters left: String - Left string. right: String - Right string. Returns A value less than zero if left precedes right , zero if they are equal, or a value greater than zero if left follows right . --- ends_with(foreign s: String, foreign suffix: String) -> bool Return whether s ends with suffix . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign suffix: String - Suffix to check. Returns true if s ends with suffix , otherwise false . --- find(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String) -> int Find the first occurrence of needle in s . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign needle: String - Substring to search for. Returns First match byte index or -1 . Notes Returns the start byte index of the first match, or -1 if no match exists. --- join(foreign parts: Vec[String], foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> String Join strings from parts with delim inserted between adjacent elements. Parameters foreign parts: Vec[String] - Input string parts. foreign delim: String - Delimiter string. r: Region - Allocation region for the joined string. Returns A newly allocated joined string. Notes If parts is empty, the function returns an empty string. --- replace(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String, foreign repl: String, region: Region) -> String Replace all occurrences of needle in s with repl . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign needle: String - Substring to replace. foreign repl: String - Replacement substring. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string with all matches replaced. Notes If needle is empty, the function returns a copy of s . --- copy(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String Copy a string into a different allocation region. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string in the destination region. --- throws slice(foreign s: String, from: int, to: int, region: Region) -> String Return a substring of s from byte index start (inclusive) to byte index end (exclusive). Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. from: int - Start byte index (inclusive). to: int - End byte index (exclusive). region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated substring. For copying slices in the source string's region, use slice syntax instead: middle := s[1:4] prefix := s[:4] suffix := s[1:] copy := s[:] These bounds are non-negative byte offsets and follow the same half-open range convention. The explicit string::slice function remains appropriate when the destination region must be selected. --- split(foreign s: String, foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> Vec[String] Split a string by a delimiter. Parameters foreign s: String - Input string. foreign delim: String - Delimiter string. r: Region - Allocation region for the output vector. Returns A vector of tokens. --- starts_with(foreign s: String, foreign prefix: String) -> bool Return whether s starts with prefix . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign prefix: String - Prefix to check. Returns true if s starts with prefix , otherwise false . --- throws to_double(s: String) -> double Convert a string to a double. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters s: String - Input string. Returns Parsed double value. --- throws to_int(s: String) -> int Convert a string to an integer. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters s: String - Input string. Returns Parsed integer value. --- trim(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String Return a copy of s with leading and trailing whitespace removed. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Trimmed string. Notes Whitespace is classified using char::isspace . Whitespace classification is byte-based."},{"title":"string: from_int(value: int, region: Region) -> String","heading":"from_int(value: int, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#from-int-value-int-region-region-string","text":"from_int(value: int, region: Region) -> String Convert an integer to a string. Parameters value: int - Integer value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated decimal string. ---"},{"title":"string: from_double(value: double, region: Region) -> String","heading":"from_double(value: double, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#from-double-value-double-region-region-string","text":"from_double(value: double, region: Region) -> String Convert a double to a string. Parameters value: double - Double value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string representation of the double. ---"},{"title":"string: from_bool(value: bool, region: Region) -> String","heading":"from_bool(value: bool, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#from-bool-value-bool-region-region-string","text":"from_bool(value: bool, region: Region) -> String Convert a boolean to a string. Parameters value: bool - Boolean value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns \"true\" or \"false\" . ---"},{"title":"string: from_char(value: char, region: Region) -> String","heading":"from_char(value: char, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#from-char-value-char-region-region-string","text":"from_char(value: char, region: Region) -> String Convert a character to a string. Parameters value: char - Character value. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated one-byte string. ---"},{"title":"string: concat(foreign left: String, foreign right: String, region: Region) -> String","heading":"concat(foreign left: String, foreign right: String, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#concat-foreign-left-string-foreign-right-string-region-region-string","text":"concat(foreign left: String, foreign right: String, region: Region) -> String Concatenate two strings. Parameters foreign left: String - Left string. foreign right: String - Right string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Concatenated string. ---"},{"title":"string: compare(foreign left: String, foreign right: String) -> int","heading":"compare(foreign left: String, foreign right: String) -> int","url":"jiklib/string.html#compare-foreign-left-string-foreign-right-string-int","text":"compare(foreign left: String, foreign right: String) -> int Compare two strings lexicographically by byte value. Parameters left: String - Left string. right: String - Right string. Returns A value less than zero if left precedes right , zero if they are equal, or a value greater than zero if left follows right . ---"},{"title":"string: ends_with(foreign s: String, foreign suffix: String) -> bool","heading":"ends_with(foreign s: String, foreign suffix: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/string.html#ends-with-foreign-s-string-foreign-suffix-string-bool","text":"ends_with(foreign s: String, foreign suffix: String) -> bool Return whether s ends with suffix . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign suffix: String - Suffix to check. Returns true if s ends with suffix , otherwise false . ---"},{"title":"string: find(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String) -> int","heading":"find(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String) -> int","url":"jiklib/string.html#find-foreign-s-string-foreign-needle-string-int","text":"find(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String) -> int Find the first occurrence of needle in s . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign needle: String - Substring to search for. Returns First match byte index or -1 . Notes Returns the start byte index of the first match, or -1 if no match exists. ---"},{"title":"string: join(foreign parts: Vec[String], foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> String","heading":"join(foreign parts: Vec[String], foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#join-foreign-parts-vec-string-foreign-delim-string-r-region-string","text":"join(foreign parts: Vec[String], foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> String Join strings from parts with delim inserted between adjacent elements. Parameters foreign parts: Vec[String] - Input string parts. foreign delim: String - Delimiter string. r: Region - Allocation region for the joined string. Returns A newly allocated joined string. Notes If parts is empty, the function returns an empty string. ---"},{"title":"string: replace(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String, foreign repl: String, region: Region) -> String","heading":"replace(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String, foreign repl: String, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#replace-foreign-s-string-foreign-needle-string-foreign-repl-string-region-region-string","text":"replace(foreign s: String, foreign needle: String, foreign repl: String, region: Region) -> String Replace all occurrences of needle in s with repl . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign needle: String - Substring to replace. foreign repl: String - Replacement substring. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string with all matches replaced. Notes If needle is empty, the function returns a copy of s . ---"},{"title":"string: copy(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String","heading":"copy(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#copy-foreign-s-string-region-region-string","text":"copy(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String Copy a string into a different allocation region. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated string in the destination region. ---"},{"title":"string: throws slice(foreign s: String, from: int, to: int, region: Region) -> String","heading":"throws slice(foreign s: String, from: int, to: int, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#throws-slice-foreign-s-string-from-int-to-int-region-region-string","text":"throws slice(foreign s: String, from: int, to: int, region: Region) -> String Return a substring of s from byte index start (inclusive) to byte index end (exclusive). Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. from: int - Start byte index (inclusive). to: int - End byte index (exclusive). region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns A newly allocated substring. For copying slices in the source string's region, use slice syntax instead: middle := s[1:4] prefix := s[:4] suffix := s[1:] copy := s[:] These bounds are non-negative byte offsets and follow the same half-open range convention. The explicit string::slice function remains appropriate when the destination region must be selected. ---"},{"title":"string: split(foreign s: String, foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> Vec[String]","heading":"split(foreign s: String, foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> Vec[String]","url":"jiklib/string.html#split-foreign-s-string-foreign-delim-string-r-region-vec-string","text":"split(foreign s: String, foreign delim: String, r: Region) -> Vec[String] Split a string by a delimiter. Parameters foreign s: String - Input string. foreign delim: String - Delimiter string. r: Region - Allocation region for the output vector. Returns A vector of tokens. ---"},{"title":"string: starts_with(foreign s: String, foreign prefix: String) -> bool","heading":"starts_with(foreign s: String, foreign prefix: String) -> bool","url":"jiklib/string.html#starts-with-foreign-s-string-foreign-prefix-string-bool","text":"starts_with(foreign s: String, foreign prefix: String) -> bool Return whether s starts with prefix . Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. foreign prefix: String - Prefix to check. Returns true if s starts with prefix , otherwise false . ---"},{"title":"string: throws to_double(s: String) -> double","heading":"throws to_double(s: String) -> double","url":"jiklib/string.html#throws-to-double-s-string-double","text":"throws to_double(s: String) -> double Convert a string to a double. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters s: String - Input string. Returns Parsed double value. ---"},{"title":"string: throws to_int(s: String) -> int","heading":"throws to_int(s: String) -> int","url":"jiklib/string.html#throws-to-int-s-string-int","text":"throws to_int(s: String) -> int Convert a string to an integer. Behavior Throws on failure. Parameters s: String - Input string. Returns Parsed integer value. ---"},{"title":"string: trim(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String","heading":"trim(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/string.html#trim-foreign-s-string-region-region-string","text":"trim(foreign s: String, region: Region) -> String Return a copy of s with leading and trailing whitespace removed. Parameters foreign s: String - Source string. region: Region - Allocation region for the result. Returns Trimmed string. Notes Whitespace is classified using char::isspace . Whitespace classification is byte-based."},{"title":"sys: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/sys.html#types","text":"Types _None._"},{"title":"sys: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/sys.html#functions","text":"Functions exit(code: int) -> void Terminate the process with a status code. Parameters code: int - Exit status. --- cwd(region: Region) -> String Return the current working directory. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Current working directory as string, or empty string on failure. --- throws executable_path(region: Region) -> String Return the absolute path of the running executable. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Absolute path of the running executable. Behavior Throws if the operating system cannot determine the executable path. Use path::dirname when locating files beside the executable. This is not the current working directory. --- getenv(name: String, region: Region) -> Option[String] Get an environment variable value. Parameters name: String - Environment variable name. region: Region Returns Value as Some[String] , or None if missing. --- platform(region: Region) -> String Return a short platform identifier string. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Platform name as string --- sleep(ms: int) -> void Sleep for a number of milliseconds (approximate). Parameters ms: int - Milliseconds. --- system(cmd: String) -> int Execute a command using the host system shell. Parameters cmd: String - Command string. Returns Command exit code (platform-dependent)."},{"title":"sys: exit(code: int) -> void","heading":"exit(code: int) -> void","url":"jiklib/sys.html#exit-code-int-void","text":"exit(code: int) -> void Terminate the process with a status code. Parameters code: int - Exit status. ---"},{"title":"sys: cwd(region: Region) -> String","heading":"cwd(region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/sys.html#cwd-region-region-string","text":"cwd(region: Region) -> String Return the current working directory. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Current working directory as string, or empty string on failure. ---"},{"title":"sys: throws executable_path(region: Region) -> String","heading":"throws executable_path(region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/sys.html#throws-executable-path-region-region-string","text":"throws executable_path(region: Region) -> String Return the absolute path of the running executable. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Absolute path of the running executable. Behavior Throws if the operating system cannot determine the executable path. Use path::dirname when locating files beside the executable. This is not the current working directory. ---"},{"title":"sys: getenv(name: String, region: Region) -> Option[String]","heading":"getenv(name: String, region: Region) -> Option[String]","url":"jiklib/sys.html#getenv-name-string-region-region-option-string","text":"getenv(name: String, region: Region) -> Option[String] Get an environment variable value. Parameters name: String - Environment variable name. region: Region Returns Value as Some[String] , or None if missing. ---"},{"title":"sys: platform(region: Region) -> String","heading":"platform(region: Region) -> String","url":"jiklib/sys.html#platform-region-region-string","text":"platform(region: Region) -> String Return a short platform identifier string. Parameters region: Region - Allocation region for the returned string. Returns Platform name as string ---"},{"title":"sys: sleep(ms: int) -> void","heading":"sleep(ms: int) -> void","url":"jiklib/sys.html#sleep-ms-int-void","text":"sleep(ms: int) -> void Sleep for a number of milliseconds (approximate). Parameters ms: int - Milliseconds. ---"},{"title":"sys: system(cmd: String) -> int","heading":"system(cmd: String) -> int","url":"jiklib/sys.html#system-cmd-string-int","text":"system(cmd: String) -> int Execute a command using the host system shell. Parameters cmd: String - Command string. Returns Command exit code (platform-dependent)."},{"title":"testing: Types","heading":"Types","url":"jiklib/testing.html#types","text":"Types TestSuite Test suite state. Fields: assertions: int - Total assertion count. failed: int - Failed assertion count. passed: int - Passed assertion count."},{"title":"testing: TestSuite","heading":"TestSuite","url":"jiklib/testing.html#testsuite","text":"TestSuite Test suite state. Fields: assertions: int - Total assertion count. failed: int - Failed assertion count. passed: int - Passed assertion count."},{"title":"testing: Functions","heading":"Functions","url":"jiklib/testing.html#functions","text":"Functions suite_new(r: Region) -> TestSuite Create a new test suite. Parameters r: Region - Allocation region for the test suite --- suite_finish(suite: TestSuite) -> void Print suite summary and exit non-zero if any test failed. Parameters suite: TestSuite - Test suite --- suite_assert(suite: TestSuite, cond: bool, s: Site) -> void Test suite assertion function. Parameters suite: TestSuite - Test suite to update. cond: bool - Assertion condition. s: Site - Source location captured for failure reporting."},{"title":"testing: suite_new(r: Region) -> TestSuite","heading":"suite_new(r: Region) -> TestSuite","url":"jiklib/testing.html#suite-new-r-region-testsuite","text":"suite_new(r: Region) -> TestSuite Create a new test suite. Parameters r: Region - Allocation region for the test suite ---"},{"title":"testing: suite_finish(suite: TestSuite) -> void","heading":"suite_finish(suite: TestSuite) -> void","url":"jiklib/testing.html#suite-finish-suite-testsuite-void","text":"suite_finish(suite: TestSuite) -> void Print suite summary and exit non-zero if any test failed. Parameters suite: TestSuite - Test suite ---"},{"title":"testing: suite_assert(suite: TestSuite, cond: bool, s: Site) -> void","heading":"suite_assert(suite: TestSuite, cond: bool, s: Site) -> void","url":"jiklib/testing.html#suite-assert-suite-testsuite-cond-bool-s-site-void","text":"suite_assert(suite: TestSuite, cond: bool, s: Site) -> void Test suite assertion function. Parameters suite: TestSuite - Test suite to update. cond: bool - Assertion condition. s: Site - Source location captured for failure reporting."},{"title":"Known Issues and Current Limitations: Current status","heading":"Current status","url":"known-issues.html#current-status","text":"Current status Jik is currently in alpha. The language is stabilizing, but some implementation details are still being refined, and some portability work remains to be completed."},{"title":"Known Issues and Current Limitations: Known limitations","heading":"Known limitations","url":"known-issues.html#known-limitations","text":"Known limitations Generated C is currently intended for clang and gcc . MSVC is not part of the default supported path. Some generated code currently relies on GNU-compatible C extensions, especially for vector repeat-initializers of the form [n of expr] . Portability work for strict non-GNU C toolchains is not complete. Some compiler command paths still construct shell command strings for host compiler and tool execution. This is a known robustness and portability limitation, especially for unusual paths, quoting edge cases, or host tool configurations."},{"title":"Known Issues and Current Limitations: Known runtime issues under review","heading":"Known runtime issues under review","url":"known-issues.html#known-runtime-issues-under-review","text":"Known runtime issues under review Region allocator arithmetic does not yet fully guard against overflow for extremely large allocation sizes. Region allocator alignment is not yet guaranteed to match alignof(max_align_t) on all host ABIs, especially for some FFI-defined C types. Scope note This page should stay short and practical. It is meant to document known user-visible limitations and important implementation issues, not to replace internal task planning documents."},{"title":"Known Issues and Current Limitations: Scope note","heading":"Scope note","url":"known-issues.html#scope-note","text":"Scope note This page should stay short and practical. It is meant to document known user-visible limitations and important implementation issues, not to replace internal task planning documents."},{"title":"Jik Language Overview: Chapters","heading":"Chapters","url":"overview.html#chapters","text":"Chapters Introduction Running Jik Code Files, Modules, and Imports Basic Syntax and Structure Values and Types Variables and Assignment Structs Memory Management in Jik Enumerations Options Variants Vectors Dictionaries Tables Control Flow Error Handling Foreign Function Interface (C interop) Builtins Standard Library --- Additional references: Jik grammar Known issues and current limitations"},{"title":"1. Introduction","heading":"1. Introduction","url":"overview/01-introduction.html#1-introduction","text":"1. Introduction Jik is a statically typed compiled language with region-based memory management, targeting C. It is a compact language which is meant to be easy to learn and easy to use. Jik is designed around explicit structure, predictable compilation, and a small language surface. Programs are translated to C, which keeps the toolchain simple and makes the generated output easy to inspect, build, and debug with existing C tooling. This overview is a guided tour of the language rather than a formal specification. Its purpose is to show how Jik code is written, what the core features look like in practice, and how the main parts of the language fit together. ---"},{"title":"2. Running Jik Code","heading":"2. Running Jik Code","url":"overview/02-running-jik-code.html#2-running-jik-code","text":"2. Running Jik Code Jik comes with a command-line tool called jik . Say we have the following Jik program: // hello.jik func main(): print(\"Hello, Jik!\") end To run the program, we do: jik run hello.jik This command: Translates all reachable Jik source files into a single C file . Compiles that C file with the C compiler given by the JIK_CC environment variable. Produces an executable and runs it. In other words, jik run is: translate -> compile -> run , in one step. Two other important commands are: jik tran hello.jik to translate the program to C without compiling it jik build hello.jik to translate and compile the program without running it For performance-oriented builds, jik tran , jik build , and jik run also support --unsafe-no-bounds-checks , which disables runtime vector bounds checks for generated v[i] reads and writes. So the three most common commands are: run : translate, compile, and run build : translate and compile tran : translate only By default, Jik uses the C compiler specified by the JIK_CC environment variable. You can also choose a compiler explicitly with the --cc option when invoking jik . To get a list of commands and help for each one: jik help For a full list of commands and options, see the Jik CLI reference . ---"},{"title":"3. Files, Modules, and Imports: 3.1 Importing modules","heading":"3.1 Importing modules","url":"overview/03-files-modules-and-imports.html#3-1-importing-modules","text":"3.1 Importing modules You import modules with use : use \"jik/testing\" as test use \"app/utils\" use \"pkg/csv\" The string is a module path which points to a .jik file with the same name Module paths always use / as the separator, on all platforms. as gives the module a short alias ( test in this example). If you omit as , the module is referred to by its basename ( utils in the example above). Import aliases must be unique within a module. This applies to both explicit as aliases and implicit basename aliases. Import aliases are local to the module containing the use declaration. Different modules may refer to the same file using different aliases; those aliases still refer to the same module symbols and types. The use syntax is the same for all module imports. The module path determines which resolver is used. 3.1.1 Local module imports Normal module imports are resolved relative to the directory of the file that contains the use declaration. use \"app/utils\" use \"shapes\" For example, if main.jik contains use \"app/utils\" , Jik looks for app/utils.jik relative to the directory containing main.jik . 3.1.2 Standard library ( jiklib ) imports Standard library modules live in jiklib , use the jik/ prefix, and are resolved relative to the jik installation directory. use \"jik/io\" use \"jik/testing\" as test These modules are documented in the standard library chapter . 3.1.3 Package imports Package modules use the pkg/ prefix and are resolved relative to the package path configured with the JIK_PKG_PATH environment variable. In the current package layout, JIK_PKG_PATH should point to a local clone of https://github.com/jik-lang/jik-packages , whose top-level packages/ directory contains package source trees. For package entry-module imports, use \"pkg/<name>\" resolves to: <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/<name>/src/<name>.jik Imports with a path after the package name resolve below that package's src/ directory: pkg/<name>/<path...> -> <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/<name>/src/<path...>.jik For example: use \"pkg/csv\" use \"pkg/csv/reader\" use \"pkg/csv/utils/foo\" resolve to: <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/csv.jik <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/reader.jik <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/utils/foo.jik During compilation, Jik resolves all modules reachable from the main module (e.g. the one which is the input to the compiler), and creates a meaningful compilation order for the modules."},{"title":"3. Files, Modules, and Imports: 3.1.1 Local module imports","heading":"3.1.1 Local module imports","url":"overview/03-files-modules-and-imports.html#3-1-1-local-module-imports","text":"3.1.1 Local module imports Normal module imports are resolved relative to the directory of the file that contains the use declaration. use \"app/utils\" use \"shapes\" For example, if main.jik contains use \"app/utils\" , Jik looks for app/utils.jik relative to the directory containing main.jik ."},{"title":"3. Files, Modules, and Imports: 3.1.2 Standard library ( jiklib ) imports","heading":"3.1.2 Standard library ( jiklib ) imports","url":"overview/03-files-modules-and-imports.html#3-1-2-standard-library-jiklib-imports","text":"3.1.2 Standard library ( jiklib ) imports Standard library modules live in jiklib , use the jik/ prefix, and are resolved relative to the jik installation directory. use \"jik/io\" use \"jik/testing\" as test These modules are documented in the standard library chapter ."},{"title":"3. Files, Modules, and Imports: 3.1.3 Package imports","heading":"3.1.3 Package imports","url":"overview/03-files-modules-and-imports.html#3-1-3-package-imports","text":"3.1.3 Package imports Package modules use the pkg/ prefix and are resolved relative to the package path configured with the JIK_PKG_PATH environment variable. In the current package layout, JIK_PKG_PATH should point to a local clone of https://github.com/jik-lang/jik-packages , whose top-level packages/ directory contains package source trees. For package entry-module imports, use \"pkg/<name>\" resolves to: <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/<name>/src/<name>.jik Imports with a path after the package name resolve below that package's src/ directory: pkg/<name>/<path...> -> <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/<name>/src/<path...>.jik For example: use \"pkg/csv\" use \"pkg/csv/reader\" use \"pkg/csv/utils/foo\" resolve to: <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/csv.jik <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/reader.jik <JIK_PKG_PATH>/packages/csv/src/utils/foo.jik During compilation, Jik resolves all modules reachable from the main module (e.g. the one which is the input to the compiler), and creates a meaningful compilation order for the modules."},{"title":"3. Files, Modules, and Imports: 3.2 Qualified names","heading":"3.2 Qualified names","url":"overview/03-files-modules-and-imports.html#3-2-qualified-names","text":"3.2 Qualified names Symbols from a module are accessed using :: : use \"jik/io\" use \"jik/testing\" as test func main(): ts := test::suite_new(_) text := must io::read_file(\"notes.txt\", _) test::suite_assert(ts, len(text) > 0, site()) end Top-level symbols from another module are accessed via module::name . This includes: functions global declarations structs enums variants For example, qualified type names such as test::TestSuite , shapes::Person , and msg::Message are valid in the same way as qualified functions such as io::read_file . Jik does not currently present separate user-visible namespaces for types versus other top-level declarations inside a module. By convention, symbols whose names start with _ are considered internal implementation details. This is not enforced by the compiler in the current language. ---"},{"title":"4. Basic Syntax and Structure: 4.1 Functions","heading":"4.1 Functions","url":"overview/04-basic-syntax-and-structure.html#4-1-functions","text":"4.1 Functions Functions are declared with func and closed with end : func add(x, y): return x + y end Arguments are written with or without types; the compiler infers / assigns C types from usages (calls) return exits the function with a value. each Jik program needs to have a main function defined, which is treated as the program entry point A version of the same function with types would look like: func add(x: int, y: int) -> int: return x + y end This is useful for writing library functions which have no usages/calls which can be used for inference by the compiler."},{"title":"4. Basic Syntax and Structure: 4.2 Blocks and control keywords","heading":"4.2 Blocks and control keywords","url":"overview/04-basic-syntax-and-structure.html#4-2-blocks-and-control-keywords","text":"4.2 Blocks and control keywords Blocks are always closed with end , for example: if ... end while ... end for ... end match ... end struct ... end variant ... end func ... end Each block introduces a new scope. However, Jik currently uses stricter redeclaration rules than C-style block shadowing: an inner local scope may not redeclare a name that already exists in an outer local scope of the same function this includes parameters and loop variables from enclosing scopes a local declaration also may not shadow a same-module global A colon : follows most control headers: if n < 0: return -n end"},{"title":"4. Basic Syntax and Structure: 4.3 Comments","heading":"4.3 Comments","url":"overview/04-basic-syntax-and-structure.html#4-3-comments","text":"4.3 Comments Use // for single-line comments: // This is a comment x = x + 1 // trailing comment"},{"title":"4. Basic Syntax and Structure: 4.4 Arithmetic and Logical operators","heading":"4.4 Arithmetic and Logical operators","url":"overview/04-basic-syntax-and-structure.html#4-4-arithmetic-and-logical-operators","text":"4.4 Arithmetic and Logical operators Arithmetic and comparison operators look as in C-style languages: x := 3 + 4 * (8 - 7) ok := (x >= 10) and (x != 13) Supported comparisons include: == , != , < , > , <= , >= . Boolean expressions use: not a a and b a or b with the usual short-circuit behavior (mirroring C). ---"},{"title":"5. Values and Types: 5.1 Primitive types","heading":"5.1 Primitive types","url":"overview/05-values-and-types.html#5-1-primitive-types","text":"5.1 Primitive types Integers : 0 , 1 , 42 , -7 , 1_000_000 type name: int translates to C type: int32_t Doubles : 3.22 , 0.5 , 3e-5 , 3.141_592 type name: double translates to C type: double _ may separate digits in integer, fractional, and exponent parts; it must appear between digits Booleans : true , false type name: bool translates to C type: bool Characters : 'a' , '+' , '[' type name: char translates to C type: unsigned char char is a single-byte value; it is not a Unicode code-point type Enumerations : State.ON , State.OFF type name: the declared enum name, such as State translates to a C enum"},{"title":"5. Values and Types: 5.2 Composite types","heading":"5.2 Composite types","url":"overview/05-values-and-types.html#5-2-composite-types","text":"5.2 Composite types Composite types are specific because they are allocated in dynamic memory. Jik handles memory management with regions, where these types are allocated. More on that topic in the next section. Strings : \"fubar\" type name: String translates to C type defined in the Jik support library strings store UTF-8 bytes ordinary string literals support \\n , \\t , \\r , \\xNN , \\\\ , \\' , and \\\" ordinary and multiline string literals may contain UTF-8 text source files that contain non-ASCII string text should be saved as UTF-8 string length, indexing, and slicing are byte-based strings are currently NUL-terminated, so ordinary string literals do not support embedded \\0 Options : Option[T] , Some{3} , None type name: Option[T] represents either a present value ( Some ) or no value ( None ) Vectors : [1, 2, 3] , [10 of 1] , Vec[Person] type name: Vec[T] translates to C type defined in the Jik support library Dictionaries : {\"foo\": 0.54, \"bar\": 2.33} , Dict[double] type name: Dict[T] string-keyed map from String keys to values of type T translates to C type defined in the Jik support library Structs type name: <struct_name> translates to C type defined in the Jik support library Variants type name: <variant_name> translates to C type defined in the Jik support library In general, capitalized type names imply a composite type, e.g. one which is allocated in a region. When referring to a user-defined type from another module, qualify it with the module alias, for example test::TestSuite , shapes::Person , or net::Message . ---"},{"title":"6. Variables and Assignment: 6.1 Declaring variables with :=","heading":"6.1 Declaring variables with :=","url":"overview/06-variables-and-assignment.html#6-1-declaring-variables-with","text":"6.1 Declaring variables with := Each variable needs to be declared and initialized with := : x := 3 name := \"Alice\" count := false Variable values can later be modified using the = operator. This syntax is useful when we want to explicitly initialize a variable upon creation. However, inside functions, we can also use a type annotation to declare a variable. In that case, the variable is given a default initial value. a: int s1: String nums: Vec[int] people: Dict[Vec[Person]] Defaults per type are: Integers : 0 Doubles : 0.0 Booleans : false Characters : 0 Strings \"\" Structs : struct with each field default initialized Vectors : empty vector of given type Dictionaries : empty dict of given type Variants : variant with active payload default initialized At top level, globals are declared with := . Typed declarations such as x: int are for local variables inside functions."},{"title":"6. Variables and Assignment: 6.2 Local scope and redeclaration rules","heading":"6.2 Local scope and redeclaration rules","url":"overview/06-variables-and-assignment.html#6-2-local-scope-and-redeclaration-rules","text":"6.2 Local scope and redeclaration rules Local variables live in block scopes, but Jik does not currently allow C-style shadowing. In practice: a local name may not be redeclared in an inner block if it already exists in an outer local scope of the same function parameters and loop variables follow the same rule a local variable also may not shadow a global declared in the same module So code such as the following is a compile error: value := 1 func main(flag: bool): if flag: value := 2 // not allowed end end"},{"title":"6. Variables and Assignment: 6.3 Binding immutability and object mutability","heading":"6.3 Binding immutability and object mutability","url":"overview/06-variables-and-assignment.html#6-3-binding-immutability-and-object-mutability","text":"6.3 Binding immutability and object mutability Immutability of a binding does not make a composite value deeply immutable. These are two different operations: assignment replaces the value held by a binding mutation changes an existing composite object through a field, vector element, or dictionary entry Global bindings are initialized once and cannot be reassigned. If a global holds a mutable composite value, however, that object's contents can still be changed: struct Box: value: int end global_box := Box{value=1} func main(): // global_box = Box{value=2} // invalid: the global binding cannot be replaced global_box.value = 2 // valid: the existing Box is mutated end"},{"title":"7. Structs: 7.1 Declaring structs","heading":"7.1 Declaring structs","url":"overview/07-structs.html#7-1-declaring-structs","text":"7.1 Declaring structs struct Config: host: String port: int verbose: bool end Field names ( host , port , verbose ) are in scope inside the struct declaration. The right-hand side gives the type of the field"},{"title":"7. Structs: 7.2 Constructing and using structs","heading":"7.2 Constructing and using structs","url":"overview/07-structs.html#7-2-constructing-and-using-structs","text":"7.2 Constructing and using structs c1 := Config{} // all fields take their default values c2 := Config{host = \"localhost\", verbose = true} // override some fields c1.port = 3 c1.host = \"foo\" Struct values can be read and updated through their fields using the usual . syntax."},{"title":"7. Structs: 7.3 Uniform function calls","heading":"7.3 Uniform function calls","url":"overview/07-structs.html#7-3-uniform-function-calls","text":"7.3 Uniform function calls A function in the same module as a struct may be called through a struct value when its first parameter has an explicit annotation for that struct type: func set_port(c: Config, port: int): c.port = port end c1.set_port(8080) // shorthand for set_port(c1, 8080) For an imported struct, the function is resolved in the module that defines the struct. This is just method-like syntactic sugar for an ordinary function call. Recursive struct cycles must pass through Option[...] . For example, next: Option[Node] is valid, while next: Node and items: Vec[Node] are compile errors. ---"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.1 The local region","heading":"8.1 The local region","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-1-the-local-region","text":"8.1 The local region Every function that allocates composite values has a local region. Composite literals without an explicit allocation target go there by default. func main(): name := \"Alice\" nums := [1, 2, 3] end Here both name and nums are allocated in main 's local region. The local region is created at function entry, and destroyed at each return point of the function. Because of that, we cannot return locally allocated values from a function. We can pass the local region to another function as an argument referred to as _ . Examples: func make_label(r: Region) -> String: return \"label\"[r] end func main(): label := make_label(_) // allocate returned String in main's local region println(label) end 8.1.1 The global region Composite globals are allocated in the global region. The global region is created at program start and remains alive until program exit. Global bindings are immutable, so a global name cannot be reassigned. A composite value held by a global binding is mutable: its members and container elements may be changed directly or by a function that receives it. All allocations owned by a global composite remain until program exit; in particular, clearing a global container does not reclaim its backing region storage. A function also cannot return a composite global directly."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.1.1 The global region","heading":"8.1.1 The global region","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-1-1-the-global-region","text":"8.1.1 The global region Composite globals are allocated in the global region. The global region is created at program start and remains alive until program exit. Global bindings are immutable, so a global name cannot be reassigned. A composite value held by a global binding is mutable: its members and container elements may be changed directly or by a function that receives it. All allocations owned by a global composite remain until program exit; in particular, clearing a global container does not reclaim its backing region storage. A function also cannot return a composite global directly."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.2 Choosing a non-local allocation target","heading":"8.2 Choosing a non-local allocation target","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-2-choosing-a-non-local-allocation-target","text":"8.2 Choosing a non-local allocation target Composite literals can also be allocated into another region: p2 := Point{}[.p1] // region of p1, where p1 is some composite value p3 := Point{}[r] // explicit region parameter r Specifically, use: [.x] to allocate into the same region as an existing composite value x [r] to allocate into a parameter r of type Region [r] and [.x] are called allocation specifiers. Allocation into a local region is only possible without a given allocation specifier, so [_] is invalid syntax. Allocation specifiers syntactically bind to their accompanying composite literal or type description. This means the first trailing [...] after a composite literal is reserved for allocation syntax such as [r] or [.x] . Because of this, composite temporaries must be bound to a name before member access, subscripting, mutation, or iteration. For example, writing t := [1, 2][0] is not allowed. Example: struct Person: name: String end func demo(p: Person): p1 := Person{name = \"Alice\"} // locally allocated p2 := Person{name = \"Bob\"}[.p] // allocated in region of \"p\" name := \"foo\"[.p] // allocated in region of \"p\" end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.3 Returning composite values","heading":"8.3 Returning composite values","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-3-returning-composite-values","text":"8.3 Returning composite values Because a function's local region is destroyed when the function returns, a function cannot safely return a composite value allocated in its own local region. So functions that return composite values usually take a destination region: struct Point: x: double y: double end func make_point(x, y, r: Region) -> Point: return Point{x = x, y = y}[r] // OK: allocated caller-provided region end func bad_point(x, y) -> Point: return Point{x = x, y = y} // ERROR: allocated in this function's local region end func main(): p1 := make_point(1.2, 0.23, _) p2 := Point{} p3 := make_point(0.01, 0.03, .p2) end This is an important pattern to remember: the caller chooses where returned composite data lives . By convention, the region parameter is usually (but need not be) the last parameter in the function."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4 The same-region rule","heading":"8.4 The same-region rule","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-the-same-region-rule","text":"8.4 The same-region rule 8.4.1 Motivation A region owns all composite values allocated into it. When a region is destroyed, all those composite values become invalid. Jik's region checks prevent a composite in one region from storing references to composite data in an incompatible region. In order for the Jik compiler to be able to check memory consistency without tracking arbitrary object graphs, it imposes certain restrictions on user code. 8.4.2 Same-region rule For a call that the compiler cannot prove is region-safe , Jik enforces the following rule: all non- foreign composite arguments and all Region arguments in one call must be in the same region This default requirement is referred to as the same-region rule . Calls to region-safe functions are a compiler-proven exception to the rule, and are described in Region-safe functions . As an example, take the following function: struct Context: id: int parent: Option[Context] end func foo(v1: Vec[Context], v2: Dict[bool], ctx: Context, r: Region): push(v1, ctx) end The function above can store ctx in v1 , so it is not region-safe, since v1 and ctx can in general be allocated in different regions. No argument is prefixed with foreign , so every call to it must pass v1 , v2 , ctx , and r from the same region. Note once again that this applies only to composite arguments. Primitive values do not participate in region checks and rules. On the other hand, if the function was defined with: func foo(foreign v1: Vec[int], foreign v2: Dict[bool], ctx: Context, r: Region) -> Context: return ctx end This function is not region-safe because it returns a composite value. Since there are multiple regions involved in the function signature, without the same-region rule, the compiler could not infer to which region the returned value belongs. Its foreign inputs are excluded from the same-region rule, so ctx and r must be in the same region. Additionally for an argument of type Region \"to be in a region\" simply means that it equals that region. Examples: struct Person: name: String end func rename(p: Person, name: String): p.name = name end func demo(p: Person): local_name := \"Local\" rename(p, local_name) // ERROR: \"p\" and \"local_name\" are in different regions rename(p, \"Alice\"[.p]) // OK: \"Alice\" is explicitly allocated in region of \"p\" rename(p, \"Alice\") // OK: compiler automatically allocates \"Alice\" in region of \"p\" end Automatic literal retargeting never selects the global region. A local literal therefore cannot be silently promoted to program-lifetime storage by a call or store operation. 8.4.3 Region-safe functions The compiler does not apply the same-region rule to a user-defined function it can prove is region-safe . A call to such a function may pass its composite and Region arguments from different regions. A function is region-safe when it cannot make one region reference a composite value from another region. In practice, this means it may inspect values, operate only on local values, or store an allocated value only into the same destination region. It must not return a composite value, store a value from one parameter region into another, store a foreign parameter, or call a function that is not itself region-safe. For example, this read-only helper is region-safe, so its caller may pass values allocated in different regions: func compare(left: String, right: String): println(left, right) end func demo(r: Region): compare(\"local\", \"other\"[r]) // OK: `compare` is region-safe end The relaxation does not permit an unsafe store. This function is not region-safe, so its callers still use the ordinary same-region rule: func append(dst: Vec[String], value: String): push(dst, value) // `value` may belong to a different caller region end If a value must be stored in another region, copy it explicitly into the destination region first: func append_copy(dst: Vec[String], value: String): push(dst, copy(value, .dst)) end This is a compiler-inferred property, not a declaration or an opt-in. If the function body later gains an unsafe operation, its calls again require the same-region rule. 8.4.4 More about foreign A composite function argument marked as foreign will not be subject to the same-region rule. The reason foreign exists is to provide a way to relax the same-region rule for arguments which do not participate in composite stores, but only need to be read from, or have their primitive contents changed. foreign should not be added only because a function reads an input. If the helper is already inferred as region-safe, the compiler already permits its arguments to come from different regions without foreign . foreign has nothing to do with mutability. As long as the contents of a foreign composite argument are primitive (for example, non-composite), they can be freely mutated. Furthermore, if multiple arguments are marked as foreign , stores between them are not allowed, as these arguments can in general come from different regions, and this would break memory consistency. Finally, returning an argument or a value rooted in a foreign -marked argument from a function is not allowed. For a function call subject to the same-region rule, a composite return value belongs to the same region as its non- foreign composite arguments and any Region arguments (returning a local value is not allowed). Allowing a foreign return would leave its region ambiguous. This allows the Jik compiler to infer in which region class a function call result is allocated - allowing foreign returns would make this impossible. 8.4.5 Consequences of the same-region rule For a call subject to the same-region rule, the compiler can safely infer that a composite return value belongs to the shared region of the function's non- foreign composite parameters and any Region parameters. A function therefore cannot return a locally allocated value, a foreign input, or a composite global. The main consequence is that the return value of a function which takes composite parameters needs to be in the same region as the shared region of the function's parameters. This gives function return values a predictable lifetime without requiring the compiler to track arbitrary object graphs. To return data in a different region, copy it explicitly into a caller-provided destination region. 8.4.6 Examples of store operations Some examples of good vs. bad store operations: func bad(y: Vec[Vec[int]]): t := [1, 2, 3] push(y, t) // ERROR: stores local composite data into caller-owned vector end func good(y: Vec[Vec[int]]): t := [1, 2, 3][.y] push(y, t) // OK, since \"y\" and \"t\" are in the same region end Composite values from different regions cannot be mixed through store operations, as this could cause memory corruption. In the function bad , we would write something stored in bad 's local region to a region outliving that region (for example, the region where y is allocated), which would be wrong. In the examples above, the compiler prevents storing local data in the region of y . Further examples: func bad_store(names: Dict[String]): local_name := \"Alice\" names[\"user\"] = local_name // ERROR: local_name is locally allocated end func good_store(names: Dict[String]): names[\"user\"] = \"Alice\"[.names] // OK: \"Alice\" is allocated in region of \"names\" names[\"user2\"] = \"Bob\" // OK: compiler allocates \"Bob\" automatically in region of \"names\" end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.1 Motivation","heading":"8.4.1 Motivation","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-1-motivation","text":"8.4.1 Motivation A region owns all composite values allocated into it. When a region is destroyed, all those composite values become invalid. Jik's region checks prevent a composite in one region from storing references to composite data in an incompatible region. In order for the Jik compiler to be able to check memory consistency without tracking arbitrary object graphs, it imposes certain restrictions on user code."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.2 Same-region rule","heading":"8.4.2 Same-region rule","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-2-same-region-rule","text":"8.4.2 Same-region rule For a call that the compiler cannot prove is region-safe , Jik enforces the following rule: all non- foreign composite arguments and all Region arguments in one call must be in the same region This default requirement is referred to as the same-region rule . Calls to region-safe functions are a compiler-proven exception to the rule, and are described in Region-safe functions . As an example, take the following function: struct Context: id: int parent: Option[Context] end func foo(v1: Vec[Context], v2: Dict[bool], ctx: Context, r: Region): push(v1, ctx) end The function above can store ctx in v1 , so it is not region-safe, since v1 and ctx can in general be allocated in different regions. No argument is prefixed with foreign , so every call to it must pass v1 , v2 , ctx , and r from the same region. Note once again that this applies only to composite arguments. Primitive values do not participate in region checks and rules. On the other hand, if the function was defined with: func foo(foreign v1: Vec[int], foreign v2: Dict[bool], ctx: Context, r: Region) -> Context: return ctx end This function is not region-safe because it returns a composite value. Since there are multiple regions involved in the function signature, without the same-region rule, the compiler could not infer to which region the returned value belongs. Its foreign inputs are excluded from the same-region rule, so ctx and r must be in the same region. Additionally for an argument of type Region \"to be in a region\" simply means that it equals that region. Examples: struct Person: name: String end func rename(p: Person, name: String): p.name = name end func demo(p: Person): local_name := \"Local\" rename(p, local_name) // ERROR: \"p\" and \"local_name\" are in different regions rename(p, \"Alice\"[.p]) // OK: \"Alice\" is explicitly allocated in region of \"p\" rename(p, \"Alice\") // OK: compiler automatically allocates \"Alice\" in region of \"p\" end Automatic literal retargeting never selects the global region. A local literal therefore cannot be silently promoted to program-lifetime storage by a call or store operation."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.3 Region-safe functions","heading":"8.4.3 Region-safe functions","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-3-region-safe-functions","text":"8.4.3 Region-safe functions The compiler does not apply the same-region rule to a user-defined function it can prove is region-safe . A call to such a function may pass its composite and Region arguments from different regions. A function is region-safe when it cannot make one region reference a composite value from another region. In practice, this means it may inspect values, operate only on local values, or store an allocated value only into the same destination region. It must not return a composite value, store a value from one parameter region into another, store a foreign parameter, or call a function that is not itself region-safe. For example, this read-only helper is region-safe, so its caller may pass values allocated in different regions: func compare(left: String, right: String): println(left, right) end func demo(r: Region): compare(\"local\", \"other\"[r]) // OK: `compare` is region-safe end The relaxation does not permit an unsafe store. This function is not region-safe, so its callers still use the ordinary same-region rule: func append(dst: Vec[String], value: String): push(dst, value) // `value` may belong to a different caller region end If a value must be stored in another region, copy it explicitly into the destination region first: func append_copy(dst: Vec[String], value: String): push(dst, copy(value, .dst)) end This is a compiler-inferred property, not a declaration or an opt-in. If the function body later gains an unsafe operation, its calls again require the same-region rule."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.4 More about foreign","heading":"8.4.4 More about foreign","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-4-more-about-foreign","text":"8.4.4 More about foreign A composite function argument marked as foreign will not be subject to the same-region rule. The reason foreign exists is to provide a way to relax the same-region rule for arguments which do not participate in composite stores, but only need to be read from, or have their primitive contents changed. foreign should not be added only because a function reads an input. If the helper is already inferred as region-safe, the compiler already permits its arguments to come from different regions without foreign . foreign has nothing to do with mutability. As long as the contents of a foreign composite argument are primitive (for example, non-composite), they can be freely mutated. Furthermore, if multiple arguments are marked as foreign , stores between them are not allowed, as these arguments can in general come from different regions, and this would break memory consistency. Finally, returning an argument or a value rooted in a foreign -marked argument from a function is not allowed. For a function call subject to the same-region rule, a composite return value belongs to the same region as its non- foreign composite arguments and any Region arguments (returning a local value is not allowed). Allowing a foreign return would leave its region ambiguous. This allows the Jik compiler to infer in which region class a function call result is allocated - allowing foreign returns would make this impossible."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.5 Consequences of the same-region rule","heading":"8.4.5 Consequences of the same-region rule","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-5-consequences-of-the-same-region-rule","text":"8.4.5 Consequences of the same-region rule For a call subject to the same-region rule, the compiler can safely infer that a composite return value belongs to the shared region of the function's non- foreign composite parameters and any Region parameters. A function therefore cannot return a locally allocated value, a foreign input, or a composite global. The main consequence is that the return value of a function which takes composite parameters needs to be in the same region as the shared region of the function's parameters. This gives function return values a predictable lifetime without requiring the compiler to track arbitrary object graphs. To return data in a different region, copy it explicitly into a caller-provided destination region."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.4.6 Examples of store operations","heading":"8.4.6 Examples of store operations","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-4-6-examples-of-store-operations","text":"8.4.6 Examples of store operations Some examples of good vs. bad store operations: func bad(y: Vec[Vec[int]]): t := [1, 2, 3] push(y, t) // ERROR: stores local composite data into caller-owned vector end func good(y: Vec[Vec[int]]): t := [1, 2, 3][.y] push(y, t) // OK, since \"y\" and \"t\" are in the same region end Composite values from different regions cannot be mixed through store operations, as this could cause memory corruption. In the function bad , we would write something stored in bad 's local region to a region outliving that region (for example, the region where y is allocated), which would be wrong. In the examples above, the compiler prevents storing local data in the region of y . Further examples: func bad_store(names: Dict[String]): local_name := \"Alice\" names[\"user\"] = local_name // ERROR: local_name is locally allocated end func good_store(names: Dict[String]): names[\"user\"] = \"Alice\"[.names] // OK: \"Alice\" is allocated in region of \"names\" names[\"user2\"] = \"Bob\" // OK: compiler allocates \"Bob\" automatically in region of \"names\" end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5 Region ergonomics and checking rules","heading":"8.5 Region ergonomics and checking rules","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-region-ergonomics-and-checking-rules","text":"8.5 Region ergonomics and checking rules For better ergonomics of everyday code, Jik introduces additional rules that make region programming more pleasant to deal with. 8.5.1 Omitted final Region argument If a function's final parameter has type Region , the caller may omit that argument. In that case, the compiler automatically inserts _ , the caller's local region. Since passing the local region is a frequent scenario, this rule makes perfect sense. Inside a global initializer, the omitted region uses the global region for that initializer. func make_label(r: Region) -> String: return \"label\"[r] end func main(): a := make_label() b := make_label(_) end Here a and b are equivalent. 8.5.2 Literal retargeting Although composite literals allocate in the local region in absence of a region specifier, Jik may retarget a local literal allocation destination when the destination region is obvious. This is what makes common calls and stores manageable. For example: struct Person: name: String age: int end func set_name(p: Person, name: String): p.name = name end func main(): p := Person{name = \"Alice\"} set_name(p, \"Bob\") // \"Bob\" is allocated in p's region p.name = \"Carol\" // \"Carol\" is allocated in p's region people := {\"Alice\": Person{}} people[\"Bob\"] = Person{name = \"Bob\"} // Person{name = \"Bob\"} is allocated in people's region v := [[1, 2], [3, 4]] push(v, [5, 6]) // [5, 6] is allocated in v's region v[0] = [0, 1, 2] // [0, 1, 2] is allocated in v's region end 8.5.3 Nested composite literals Composite literals that contain other composite values must be internally region-consistent. The outer value and the contained composite values must belong to the same region. This applies to: vector elements vector repeated initializers dictionary values struct initializer fields variant payloads Some payloads For container literals, only an outer region specifier is possible. Hence, if container contents are literals, these are automatically retargeted to the destination marked by the container's allocation specifier. For example: func foo(v: Vec[String]): a := [\"bar\", \"baz\"][.v] // \"bar\" and \"baz\" are automatically allocated in the region of v x := \"local\" y := [\"foo\", x][.v] // compile error: x is local, but the vector is allocated in v's region end 8.5.4 Temporary containers passed to foreign parameters A temporary Vec or Dict literal passed directly to a foreign parameter may contain composite elements from different regions. This exception applies only in that foreign argument position; ordinary container literals must still be internally region-consistent. For example: use \"jik/process\" func foo(args: Vec[String], foreign cmd: String): res := must process::capture(cmd, [ \"foo\", \"bar\", args[0] ], _ ) end This works because the signature of process::capture is: throws func capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result Since args in process::capture is a foreign vector, at the call site the elements of the vector literal (e.g. the strings) need not be in the same region. 8.5.5 Region-safe builtins All currently provided builtins except push are region-safe, so their calls do not need to satisfy the same-region rule. Some only inspect their composite arguments; others, such as concat and copy , explicitly identify the destination region for values they allocate. push is checked as a store operation because it can retain its value argument in a vector. A push of a primitive value is region-safe; for a composite value, the compiler applies the usual store-safety checks. concat is still explicit about its destination region: its last argument must be a Region , and the returned String is allocated there. copy is also explicit about its destination region, but unlike ordinary calls its source value may come from any region. func show(foreign left: String, right: String, r: Region): println(left, right) joined := concat(left, right, r) left_copy := copy(left, r) end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5.1 Omitted final Region argument","heading":"8.5.1 Omitted final Region argument","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-1-omitted-final-region-argument","text":"8.5.1 Omitted final Region argument If a function's final parameter has type Region , the caller may omit that argument. In that case, the compiler automatically inserts _ , the caller's local region. Since passing the local region is a frequent scenario, this rule makes perfect sense. Inside a global initializer, the omitted region uses the global region for that initializer. func make_label(r: Region) -> String: return \"label\"[r] end func main(): a := make_label() b := make_label(_) end Here a and b are equivalent."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5.2 Literal retargeting","heading":"8.5.2 Literal retargeting","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-2-literal-retargeting","text":"8.5.2 Literal retargeting Although composite literals allocate in the local region in absence of a region specifier, Jik may retarget a local literal allocation destination when the destination region is obvious. This is what makes common calls and stores manageable. For example: struct Person: name: String age: int end func set_name(p: Person, name: String): p.name = name end func main(): p := Person{name = \"Alice\"} set_name(p, \"Bob\") // \"Bob\" is allocated in p's region p.name = \"Carol\" // \"Carol\" is allocated in p's region people := {\"Alice\": Person{}} people[\"Bob\"] = Person{name = \"Bob\"} // Person{name = \"Bob\"} is allocated in people's region v := [[1, 2], [3, 4]] push(v, [5, 6]) // [5, 6] is allocated in v's region v[0] = [0, 1, 2] // [0, 1, 2] is allocated in v's region end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5.3 Nested composite literals","heading":"8.5.3 Nested composite literals","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-3-nested-composite-literals","text":"8.5.3 Nested composite literals Composite literals that contain other composite values must be internally region-consistent. The outer value and the contained composite values must belong to the same region. This applies to: vector elements vector repeated initializers dictionary values struct initializer fields variant payloads Some payloads For container literals, only an outer region specifier is possible. Hence, if container contents are literals, these are automatically retargeted to the destination marked by the container's allocation specifier. For example: func foo(v: Vec[String]): a := [\"bar\", \"baz\"][.v] // \"bar\" and \"baz\" are automatically allocated in the region of v x := \"local\" y := [\"foo\", x][.v] // compile error: x is local, but the vector is allocated in v's region end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5.4 Temporary containers passed to foreign parameters","heading":"8.5.4 Temporary containers passed to foreign parameters","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-4-temporary-containers-passed-to-foreign-parameters","text":"8.5.4 Temporary containers passed to foreign parameters A temporary Vec or Dict literal passed directly to a foreign parameter may contain composite elements from different regions. This exception applies only in that foreign argument position; ordinary container literals must still be internally region-consistent. For example: use \"jik/process\" func foo(args: Vec[String], foreign cmd: String): res := must process::capture(cmd, [ \"foo\", \"bar\", args[0] ], _ ) end This works because the signature of process::capture is: throws func capture(foreign program: String, foreign args: Vec[String], region: Region) -> Result Since args in process::capture is a foreign vector, at the call site the elements of the vector literal (e.g. the strings) need not be in the same region."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.5.5 Region-safe builtins","heading":"8.5.5 Region-safe builtins","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-5-5-region-safe-builtins","text":"8.5.5 Region-safe builtins All currently provided builtins except push are region-safe, so their calls do not need to satisfy the same-region rule. Some only inspect their composite arguments; others, such as concat and copy , explicitly identify the destination region for values they allocate. push is checked as a store operation because it can retain its value argument in a vector. A push of a primitive value is region-safe; for a composite value, the compiler applies the usual store-safety checks. concat is still explicit about its destination region: its last argument must be a Region , and the returned String is allocated there. copy is also explicit about its destination region, but unlike ordinary calls its source value may come from any region. func show(foreign left: String, right: String, r: Region): println(left, right) joined := concat(left, right, r) left_copy := copy(left, r) end"},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.6 Copying values between regions","heading":"8.6 Copying values between regions","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-6-copying-values-between-regions","text":"8.6 Copying values between regions Use the builtin copy to explicitly copy specific composite values into a chosen destination region: copy(value, region) -> T copy(value) -> T // allocated in the local region A concrete example: func foo(v: Vec[int]): x := [1, 2, 3] x_new = copy(x, .v) // copy x to the region of v v_new = copy(v, _) // copy v to the local region return x_new end Supported copyable types are: String Vec[P] Dict[P] Option[P] structs whose fields are all P variants whose every payload type is P Here P means int , double , bool , char , String , or an enum type. Strings are primitive-like for this rule, but copied strings are freshly allocated in the destination region. Top-level primitives and enums are rejected because they are not composite: copy(12, _) // compile error copy(State.ON, _) // compile error Extern structs and nested composites such as Vec[Vec[int]] or a struct field of type Vec[String] are not supported."},{"title":"8. Memory management in Jik: 8.7 Summary","heading":"8.7 Summary","url":"overview/08-memory-management.html#8-7-summary","text":"8.7 Summary If you keep these rules in mind, the Jik region model is fairly straightforward: Composite values live in regions. _ is the current function's local region. Functions that return composite values usually take a destination Region . Calls to functions whose final parameter has type Region may omit that argument. When omitted, the caller's local region is passed automatically. Composite calls and stores must preserve region consistency, except calls to compiler-proven region-safe functions. Use foreign for read-oriented inputs from other regions."},{"title":"9. Enumerations","heading":"9. Enumerations","url":"overview/09-enumerations.html#9-enumerations","text":"9. Enumerations Enumerations define a type whose values come from a fixed set of named cases. They are useful when you want to model a small closed set of states or options. An enumeration is defined like: enum State: ON OFF end And used as follows: s1 := State.ON s2 := State.OFF assert(s1 != s2) assert(s1 == State.ON) Enumeration values are qualified with the enum name, so State.ON and State.OFF are distinct values of the type State . Enums work naturally with control flow: func is_on(s): if s == State.ON: return true end return false end When code needs to handle every possible enum value, use an exhaustive match . Every enum member must be handled exactly once, so adding a new member makes affected matches fail at compile time until they are updated. func signal_name(s: State, r: Region) -> String: match s: case State.ON: return \"on\"[r] case State.OFF: return \"off\"[r] end end Enum cases must be qualified, including for imported enums. case State.ON: is valid; bare case ON: is not. Use enums when the possible values are known in advance and do not need to carry additional data. If each case needs associated values, variants are the more general construct. A table can associate one stored value with every member of an enum. ---"},{"title":"10. Options: 10.1 Creating options","heading":"10.1 Creating options","url":"overview/10-options.html#10-1-creating-options","text":"10.1 Creating options An option has two states: Some{value} means a value is present None means no value is present Examples: a := Some{3} b: Option[int] c: Option[String] a has type Option[int] . Both b and c default to None . When writing None directly, the payload type must be known from context: x: Option[int] x = None"},{"title":"10. Options: 10.2 Checking options","heading":"10.2 Checking options","url":"overview/10-options.html#10-2-checking-options","text":"10.2 Checking options Use is Some and is None : item := Some{\"hello\"} if item is Some: println(\"present\") end if item is None: println(\"missing\") end These checks always produce bool ."},{"title":"10. Options: 10.3 Extracting the payload","heading":"10.3 Extracting the payload","url":"overview/10-options.html#10-3-extracting-the-payload","text":"10.3 Extracting the payload If x has type Option[T] , then x? has type T : item := Some{7} println(item?) Applying ? to None is a runtime error, so it is usually used after an is Some check when absence is possible. Another way to extract the payload is to use the builtin function unwrap , so unwrap(x) is equivalent to x? ."},{"title":"10. Options: 10.4 Common uses","heading":"10.4 Common uses","url":"overview/10-options.html#10-4-common-uses","text":"10.4 Common uses Options are the natural way to model: dictionary lookup results recursive links such as next: Option[Node] Examples: ages: Dict[int] ages[\"Alice\"] = 31 if ages[\"Alice\"] is Some: println(ages[\"Alice\"]?) end struct Node: next: Option[Node] end Recursive struct and variant cycles must pass through Option[...] . Direct cycles such as next: Node or items: Vec[Node] are compile errors. ---"},{"title":"11. Variants: 11.1 Declaring variants","heading":"11.1 Declaring variants","url":"overview/11-variants.html#11-1-declaring-variants","text":"11.1 Declaring variants Examples: variant Value: INT: int TEXT: String NUMS: Vec[int] EOF end"},{"title":"11. Variants: 11.2 Constructing variants","heading":"11.2 Constructing variants","url":"overview/11-variants.html#11-2-constructing-variants","text":"11.2 Constructing variants v1 := Value.INT{7} // If initial value is not given, it is given the default initializer value for that type // For this case, it is 0 v2 := Value.INT{} v3 := Value.TEXT{\"hello\"} v4 := Value.NUMS{[10 of 0]} v5 := Value.EOF"},{"title":"11. Variants: 11.3 Inspecting, extracting and changing tags","heading":"11.3 Inspecting, extracting and changing tags","url":"overview/11-variants.html#11-3-inspecting-extracting-and-changing-tags","text":"11.3 Inspecting, extracting and changing tags Use is to check which variant tag is active. Following up on the declarations above: assert(v1 is Value.INT) assert(v2 is Value.INT) assert(v3 is Value.TEXT) assert(v4 is Value.NUMS) assert(v5 is Value.EOF) Payload extraction is done with an index-like syntax: nums := v4[Value.NUMS] assert(nums[0] == 0) This reads as \"treat this value as the Value.NUMS case and give me its payload\". In the translated C code, it is checked if the active tag is accessed or not. In the latter case, a runtime error is thrown. We can modify a variant instance by setting another tag as active: v3 = Value.INT{2} assert(v3 is Value.INT)"},{"title":"11. Variants: 11.4 Pattern matching on variants","heading":"11.4 Pattern matching on variants","url":"overview/11-variants.html#11-4-pattern-matching-on-variants","text":"11.4 Pattern matching on variants Jik supports match for more concise handling of variants and enums. A variant match uses tag patterns and can bind payloads: func handle(val): match val: case Value.INT{v}: print(\"INT: \", v) case Value.TEXT{msg}: print(\"TEXT: \", msg) case Value.NUMS{vec}: print(\"NUMS: \", vec) case Value.EOF: print(\"end of input\") end end Each case : Tests the tag Binds the payload to a local name ( v , msg , vec ). Tags may omit their payload type. Construct and match those tags without braces, as with Value.EOF ; braces always indicate a payload. If every tag has no payload, declare an enum instead of a variant . When printed, variants show their type, active tag, and payload when present: for example, <Value INT=7> and <Value EOF> . It is important to note that match is exhaustive, which means it requires every variant tag or enum member to be handled by a respective case , otherwise there is a compile error. Variants can be placed freely into vectors, dictionaries, and other structs, and behave as regular values. Recursive cycles involving variant payloads must also pass through Option[...] . Payloads such as Expr , Vec[Expr] , or Dict[Expr] that recurse back without Option are compile errors."},{"title":"11. Variants: 11.5 Uniform function calls","heading":"11.5 Uniform function calls","url":"overview/11-variants.html#11-5-uniform-function-calls","text":"11.5 Uniform function calls A function in the same module as a variant may be called through a value of that variant when its first parameter has an explicit annotation for the variant type: func is_text(value: Value) -> bool: return value is Value.TEXT end v := Value.TEXT{\"hello\"} assert(v.is_text()) // shorthand for is_text(v) For an imported variant, the function is resolved in the module that defines the variant, just as it is for an imported struct. A table can associate one stored value with every variant tag. Such a lookup considers only the active tag and does not inspect its payload. ---"},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.1 Vectors initialized from type","heading":"12.1 Vectors initialized from type","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-1-vectors-initialized-from-type","text":"12.1 Vectors initialized from type // create an empty vector of integers, which is initialized to an empty // vector which holds type int with default initializer value v: Vec[int]"},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.2 Vectors initialized with a repeat initializer","heading":"12.2 Vectors initialized with a repeat initializer","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-2-vectors-initialized-with-a-repeat-initializer","text":"12.2 Vectors initialized with a repeat initializer // create a vector of 10 Persons with name \"foo\" v := [10 of Person{name=\"foo\"}] The form [n of value] creates a vector of length n , with each element initialized from value ."},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.3 Vectors initialized from arbitrary initializer","heading":"12.3 Vectors initialized from arbitrary initializer","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-3-vectors-initialized-from-arbitrary-initializer","text":"12.3 Vectors initialized from arbitrary initializer v := [false, true, false]"},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.4 Basic operations with vectors","heading":"12.4 Basic operations with vectors","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-4-basic-operations-with-vectors","text":"12.4 Basic operations with vectors Examples. numbers: Vec[int] push(numbers, 1) push(numbers, 2) assert(len(numbers) == 2) numbers[1] = 3 assert(pop(numbers) == 3) assert(len(numbers) == 1) push , pop and len are builtin functions which work on vectors and dictionaries."},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.5 Slicing vectors","heading":"12.5 Slicing vectors","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-5-slicing-vectors","text":"12.5 Slicing vectors Vectors support copying slices with half-open bounds: numbers := [10, 20, 30, 40] middle := numbers[1:3] // [20, 30] prefix := numbers[:2] // [10, 20] suffix := numbers[2:] // [30, 40] copy := numbers[:] Slices return a new vector allocated in the source vector's region. Bounds are non-negative integers; invalid bounds abort at runtime. Slice syntax is only available for vectors and strings, and slices cannot be assigned to."},{"title":"12. Vectors: 12.6 Iterating over vector elements","heading":"12.6 Iterating over vector elements","url":"overview/12-vectors.html#12-6-iterating-over-vector-elements","text":"12.6 Iterating over vector elements One can iterate over vector elements with a for loop: chars := ['a', 'b', 'c'] for ch in chars: if ch == 'd': print(\"d found\") end end One can also enumerate the iteration as follows: for i, ch in chars: print(i, \": \", ch) end"},{"title":"13. Dictionaries: 13.1 Creating dictionaries","heading":"13.1 Creating dictionaries","url":"overview/13-dictionaries.html#13-1-creating-dictionaries","text":"13.1 Creating dictionaries Like vectors, we can create empty dictionaries from type descriptions: counts: Dict[int] buckets: Dict[Vec[String]] Also, we can directly specify the initial key-value pairs: config := { \"host\": \"localhost\", \"port\": 8080, } commands := { \"copy\": {\"overwrite\": true, \"recursive\": false}, \"move\": {\"overwrite\": true, \"recursive\": true}, }"},{"title":"13. Dictionaries: 13.2 Dictionary lookup","heading":"13.2 Dictionary lookup","url":"overview/13-dictionaries.html#13-2-dictionary-lookup","text":"13.2 Dictionary lookup Access returns an Option[T] , where T is the dictionary value type. Example: counts: Dict[int] counts[\"apple\"] = 3 counts[\"banana\"] = 5 item := counts[\"apple\"] if item is Some: print(item?) end missing := counts[\"orange\"] if missing is None: print(\"no oranges found\") end Forced lookup is concise with ? : print(counts[\"apple\"]?) This throws a runtime error if the key is missing."},{"title":"13. Dictionaries: 13.3 Dictionary iteration","heading":"13.3 Dictionary iteration","url":"overview/13-dictionaries.html#13-3-dictionary-iteration","text":"13.3 Dictionary iteration This is achieved using a for loop: for k, v in counts: print(\"key = \", k, \", value = \", v) end With two names, vectors bind index, value , while dictionaries bind key, value . ---"},{"title":"14. Tables","heading":"14. Tables","url":"overview/14-tables.html#14-tables","text":"14. Tables Tables are fixed, module-level associations from every member of an enum, or every tag of a variant, to a value of one declared type. Their entries are initialized once and cannot be replaced. enum Opcode: PUSH POP PRINT end table StackDelta[Opcode] -> int: PUSH: 1 POP: -1 PRINT: 0 end delta := StackDelta[Opcode.PUSH] Each member must appear exactly once. Unknown members, duplicate entries, and missing entries are compile errors. There is no default entry, so adding an enum member also requires updating every table keyed by that enum. Source entry order is irrelevant. A variant-tag table uses the active tag and ignores its payload: variant Token: IDENT: String NUMBER: int END end table TokenNames[Token] -> String: IDENT: \"identifier\" NUMBER: \"number\" END: \"end\" end token := Token.IDENT{\"name\"} name := TokenNames[token] Entry expressions are evaluated once before global initialization. Composite entries therefore have global lifetime and are shared: looking up the same entry does not construct or copy it. Use copy(TableName[key], region) when an independent composite is required. As with globals, this is binding immutability rather than deep object immutability . Table entries cannot call functions, including built-ins, or read global variables. They may look up an earlier table. Tables are initialized before globals, so global initializers may look up a table. A table entry cannot look up itself or a table initialized later. Imported tables use normal module qualification: label := protocol::OpcodeNames[opcode] Tables support lookup only. Their entries cannot be reassigned, and tables cannot be passed as values, iterated, queried with len , or used as types. A lookup of a composite entry still returns the shared composite value, so its contents may be mutated; use copy when an independent value is required. Unlike Dict , a table has a fixed enum or variant key set, is checked for completeness at compile time, and never returns Option[T] ."},{"title":"15. Control Flow: 15.1 if / elif / else","heading":"15.1 if / elif / else","url":"overview/15-control-flow.html#15-1-if-elif-else","text":"15.1 if / elif / else func is_less_than_zero(x): if x < 0: return true elif x == 0: return false else: return false end end elif and else are both optional, and one can have multiple elif branches. Jik also supports an expression form for simple conditional selection: t := 2 if foo == 3 else 12 name := \"yes\"[r] if flag else \"no\"[r] Both branches must produce compatible value types, and the middle condition must be bool ."},{"title":"15. Control Flow: 15.2 while loops","heading":"15.2 while loops","url":"overview/15-control-flow.html#15-2-while-loops","text":"15.2 while loops func sum_to(n): i := 0 s := 0 while i <= n: s += i i += 1 end return s end The loop condition is re-evaluated on each iteration; break and continue keywords are also supported, as in other iteration constructs."},{"title":"15. Control Flow: 15.3 for loops","heading":"15.3 for loops","url":"overview/15-control-flow.html#15-3-for-loops","text":"15.3 for loops Jik has two for patterns: numeric ranges and vector / dictionary iteration. Numeric for : n := 100 for i = 0, n: // do something end Container iteration: // Vector iteration points := [3 of Point{x = 2}] for p in points: print(p.y - p.x) end // Dictionary iteration config := {\"foo\": 12, \"bar\": 1} for key, value in config: print(key, \": \", value) end ---"},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.1 Declaring a throwing function","heading":"16.1 Declaring a throwing function","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-1-declaring-a-throwing-function","text":"16.1 Declaring a throwing function A function which may fail must be declared with throws func : throws func div_safe(x, y): if y == 0: fail(\"division by 0\") end return x / y end Inside such a function, fail(...) marks the operation as failed. fail(msg) uses the default error code 1 , while fail(msg, code) lets you set an explicit integer error code. fail(...) also terminates the current control-flow path. Any statement that follows fail(...) in the same block is unreachable and is a compile error."},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.2 Handling failures with try / except","heading":"16.2 Handling failures with try / except","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-2-handling-failures-with-try-except","text":"16.2 Handling failures with try / except A throwing call can be wrapped in a try block: try x := div_safe(3, 0): print(\"ok: \", x) except: print(\"error\") end If the call succeeds, execution continues in the try body. If it fails, the except branch runs instead. The variable introduced in try x := ... is only valid in the try body. Referring to it inside except or after the entire try/except/end construct is a compile error. If the throwing function returns nothing, we simply write: try foo(): print(\"OK\") except: print(\"err\") end"},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.3 Propagating failures","heading":"16.3 Propagating failures","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-3-propagating-failures","text":"16.3 Propagating failures Within a throws func , try can pass a failure to the caller instead of handling it locally. It can be used with either a declaration or a call that does not return a value: throws func half_of_safe_value(): x := try div_safe(10, 2) return x / 2 end throws func save_record(record): try write_record(record) end The value declared by x := try ... is in the enclosing scope. In either form, if the call fails, the function returns through its error path before any following statement runs. The propagation form has no trailing colon; a colon starts a local try ... except ... end handler."},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.4 Requiring success with must","heading":"16.4 Requiring success with must","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-4-requiring-success-with-must","text":"16.4 Requiring success with must When a failure does not need to be handled separately, use must : x := must div_safe(10, 2) must calls a throwing function and panics if the call results in an error. The postfix form call()! has the same behavior and is convenient within expressions: x := div_safe(10, 2)! print(div_safe(10, 2)! == 5) ! applies only to function calls. Use try ... except ... end when failure needs local recovery."},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.5 Inspecting the current error","heading":"16.5 Inspecting the current error","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-5-inspecting-the-current-error","text":"16.5 Inspecting the current error Inside an except block, the current error can be inspected with: error_msg([Region]) -> String error_code() -> int error_msg() may be called with no argument, in which case the local region _ is used. Example: try result := div_safe(10, 0): println(\"result = \", result) except: println(\"failed: \", error_msg()) println(\"code = \", error_code()) end"},{"title":"16. Error handling: 16.6 Example","heading":"16.6 Example","url":"overview/16-error-handling.html#16-6-example","text":"16.6 Example throws func div_safe(x, y): if y == 0: fail(\"division by 0\") end return x / y end func main(): try result := div_safe(10, 0): println(\"result = \", result) except: println(\"division failed\") end ok := must div_safe(10, 2) println(ok) end ---"},{"title":"17. Foreign Function Interface (C interop): 17.1 Extern functions","heading":"17.1 Extern functions","url":"overview/17-ffi-c-interop.html#17-1-extern-functions","text":"17.1 Extern functions Extern functions are declared like so: extern func impl_adder as adder(x: int, y: int) -> int This assumes that impl_adder is either available through the Jik support library ( core.h ), or that it is embedded within Jik code, like so: @embed{C_END} int32_t impl_adder(int32_t x, int32_t y) { return x + y; } C_END Here, C_END is a user-defined label which marks the end of embedded C code. The idea is that this label should not be contained in any of the C code, so it can correctly be processed by the Jik compiler. One can then use it like any other function: assert(adder(3, 2) == 5)"},{"title":"17. Foreign Function Interface (C interop): 17.2 Extern structs","heading":"17.2 Extern structs","url":"overview/17-ffi-c-interop.html#17-2-extern-structs","text":"17.2 Extern structs Regarding structs, here is an example: extern struct impl_Point as Point extern func impl_Point_new as point_new(x: double, y: double, region: Region) -> Point extern func impl_Point_x as point_x(p: Point) -> double @embed{C_END} typedef struct impl_Point { double x; double y; } impl_Point; impl_Point * impl_Point_new(double x, double y, JikRegion *r) { impl_Point *p = jik_region_alloc(r, sizeof(impl_Point)); p->x = x; p->y = y; return p; } double impl_Point_x(impl_Point *p) { return p->x; } C_END Typically, when creating composite objects, we need to pass a region where they should be allocated. Extern structs cannot be read or modified the same way as non-extern structs, since they are opaque. Field access and mutation must be exposed through a specifically constructed API, as demonstrated above."},{"title":"17. Foreign Function Interface (C interop): 17.3 Default initialization for extern structs","heading":"17.3 Default initialization for extern structs","url":"overview/17-ffi-c-interop.html#17-3-default-initialization-for-extern-structs","text":"17.3 Default initialization for extern structs Some extern structs have a meaningful empty value. These can declare one extern function as the type's default initializer: extern struct impl_Buffer as Buffer extern init func impl_Buffer_new as new(region: Region) -> Buffer An extern init func must: return an extern struct type take exactly one Region parameter not be throws be the only init function declared for that extern struct type The public function name is not special. new is only a convention; the init marker is what makes the function a default initializer. When an extern struct has an init function, default construction uses it: b1: Buffer b2 := Buffer{} Both declarations above are lowered as if they called the marked init function with the current allocation region: b := new(_) Only empty construction is allowed. Extern structs are still opaque, so a fielded construction such as Buffer{x = 1} is invalid. Extern structs without an init function cannot be default-constructed: f: io::File // error because io::File has no extern init function"},{"title":"17. Foreign Function Interface (C interop): 17.4 Generated C names and identifier restrictions","heading":"17.4 Generated C names and identifier restrictions","url":"overview/17-ffi-c-interop.html#17-4-generated-c-names-and-identifier-restrictions","text":"17.4 Generated C names and identifier restrictions Jik reserves identifiers beginning with jik_ , Jik , and JIK . Do not use these prefixes for functions, types, globals, locals, fields, module aliases, or other Jik identifiers. Jik emits local variables and struct fields using their source spelling. To keep the generated C valid, names reserved by the C backend are also rejected in those positions. This includes C keywords, implementation-reserved names such as __name and _Name , and common names from the C headers included by Jik, such as size_t , FILE , EOF , and NULL . Generated C names are implementation details, not a stable C API. In particular, the compiler currently uses these namespaces: jik_src_* symbols derived from Jik source declarations jik_vec_* generated vector types and helpers jik_dict_* generated dictionary types and helpers jik_opt_* generated option types and helpers jik_* compiler and runtime helpers Jik* runtime C types Embedded C is passed through unchanged and may introduce its own names or C macros. It must therefore avoid collisions with Jik-generated names and follow the normal C rules itself."},{"title":"17. Foreign Function Interface (C interop): 17.5 Native build requirements","heading":"17.5 Native build requirements","url":"overview/17-ffi-c-interop.html#17-5-native-build-requirements","text":"17.5 Native build requirements An FFI module can declare the include paths, library paths, libraries, and runtime files needed to build programs that import it: @includedir(\"../vendor/raylib/include\") @platform(windows) @profile(windows-x64-mingw) @libdir(\"../vendor/raylib/lib/windows\") @link(\"raylib\", \"gdi32\", \"winmm\") @copy(\"../vendor/raylib/bin/windows/raylib.dll\") @platform(linux) @profile(linux-x64-gnu) @libdir(\"../vendor/raylib/lib/linux\") @link(\"raylib\", \"m\", \"pthread\", \"dl\") Every module begins in platform all . A @platform directive selects the platform for later build directives in the same physical file; @platform(all) restores platform-independent behavior. @profile additionally selects the native compiler target required by later build directives: @profile(windows-x64-mingw) for MinGW on Windows or @profile(linux-x64-gnu) for GNU toolchains on Linux. @profile(all) removes this restriction. A directive guarded by a profile is active only when both its platform and profile match; this lets an FFI module reject an incompatible native toolchain before compilation. MSVC is not currently a supported native build profile. Relative paths are resolved from the file containing the directive. Requirements are collected transitively from every reachable module. @includedir adds header search directories, @libdir adds library search directories, @link links named libraries, and @copy copies files beside the executable after a successful compilation. Each directive accepts one or more comma-separated string arguments. ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: 18.1 Output","heading":"18.1 Output","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#18-1-output","text":"18.1 Output print(...) -> void Takes any number of arguments and writes each argument to the standard output using the argument's default textual representation. Each argument can be of any type. Options print as <Some: val> or <None[T]> , for example: println(Some{12}) println(None[String]) println(...) -> void Same as print , and additionally writes a newline after the arguments. concat(...String, Region) -> String Concatenates one or more strings and allocates the result in the final Region argument. The region may be omitted, in which case Jik uses the local region _ . All string arguments must have type String . When supplied, the final argument must be a Region . Example msg := concat(\"hello\", \", \", name, \"!\") same := concat(\"hello\", \", \", name, \"!\", _) ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: print(...) -> void","heading":"print(...) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#print-void","text":"print(...) -> void Takes any number of arguments and writes each argument to the standard output using the argument's default textual representation. Each argument can be of any type. Options print as <Some: val> or <None[T]> , for example: println(Some{12}) println(None[String])"},{"title":"18. Builtins: println(...) -> void","heading":"println(...) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#println-void","text":"println(...) -> void Same as print , and additionally writes a newline after the arguments."},{"title":"18. Builtins: concat(...String, Region) -> String","heading":"concat(...String, Region) -> String","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#concat-string-region-string","text":"concat(...String, Region) -> String Concatenates one or more strings and allocates the result in the final Region argument. The region may be omitted, in which case Jik uses the local region _ . All string arguments must have type String . When supplied, the final argument must be a Region . Example msg := concat(\"hello\", \", \", name, \"!\") same := concat(\"hello\", \", \", name, \"!\", _) ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: 18.2 Container builtins","heading":"18.2 Container builtins","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#18-2-container-builtins","text":"18.2 Container builtins push(Vec[T], T) -> void Pushes a value of type T to the end of a vector of type Vec[T] . Parameters Vector Value to push v := [1, 2] push(v, 10) pop(Vec[T]) -> T Pops the last element from a vector of type Vec[T] . Parameters Vector Returns A value of type T . Example v := [1, 2, 3] assert(pop(v) == 3) len(T) -> int Returns the length of a container or string. Parameters Value of type Vec , Dict or String Returns Length of the container or string Example n = len(v) m = len(s) clear(T) -> void Resets the size of a vector or dictionary to 0 and does not free memory. Parameters Value of type Vec or Dict Example d := {\"foo\": 12, \"bar\": 13} clear(d) The container builtins in this section also support uniform function-call syntax: v.push(value) , v.pop() , v.len() , and v.clear() are equivalent to calls with v as their first argument. ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: push(Vec[T], T) -> void","heading":"push(Vec[T], T) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#push-vec-t-t-void","text":"push(Vec[T], T) -> void Pushes a value of type T to the end of a vector of type Vec[T] . Parameters Vector Value to push v := [1, 2] push(v, 10)"},{"title":"18. Builtins: pop(Vec[T]) -> T","heading":"pop(Vec[T]) -> T","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#pop-vec-t-t","text":"pop(Vec[T]) -> T Pops the last element from a vector of type Vec[T] . Parameters Vector Returns A value of type T . Example v := [1, 2, 3] assert(pop(v) == 3)"},{"title":"18. Builtins: len(T) -> int","heading":"len(T) -> int","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#len-t-int","text":"len(T) -> int Returns the length of a container or string. Parameters Value of type Vec , Dict or String Returns Length of the container or string Example n = len(v) m = len(s)"},{"title":"18. Builtins: clear(T) -> void","heading":"clear(T) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#clear-t-void","text":"clear(T) -> void Resets the size of a vector or dictionary to 0 and does not free memory. Parameters Value of type Vec or Dict Example d := {\"foo\": 12, \"bar\": 13} clear(d) The container builtins in this section also support uniform function-call syntax: v.push(value) , v.pop() , v.len() , and v.clear() are equivalent to calls with v as their first argument. ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: 18.3 Debugging","heading":"18.3 Debugging","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#18-3-debugging","text":"18.3 Debugging Debugging builtins return information about a call site. Basic debugging information is obtained through the opaque type Site . site() -> Site Returns an opaque structured representation of the current site information. Returns value of type Site site_file(Site, Region) -> String Returns the source file path/name for the current site, as a string allocated in the given region. Parameters Site Region to allocate the filepath Returns filepath site_line(Site) -> int Returns the source line number for the current site. Parameters Site Returns line number site_code(Site, Region) -> String Returns the Jik source code line for the current site, as a string allocated in the given region. Parameters Site Region to allocate the codeline Returns code line Example s := site() println(site_file(s, _), site_line(s), site_code(s, _)) ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: site() -> Site","heading":"site() -> Site","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#site-site","text":"site() -> Site Returns an opaque structured representation of the current site information. Returns value of type Site"},{"title":"18. Builtins: site_file(Site, Region) -> String","heading":"site_file(Site, Region) -> String","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#site-file-site-region-string","text":"site_file(Site, Region) -> String Returns the source file path/name for the current site, as a string allocated in the given region. Parameters Site Region to allocate the filepath Returns filepath"},{"title":"18. Builtins: site_line(Site) -> int","heading":"site_line(Site) -> int","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#site-line-site-int","text":"site_line(Site) -> int Returns the source line number for the current site. Parameters Site Returns line number"},{"title":"18. Builtins: site_code(Site, Region) -> String","heading":"site_code(Site, Region) -> String","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#site-code-site-region-string","text":"site_code(Site, Region) -> String Returns the Jik source code line for the current site, as a string allocated in the given region. Parameters Site Region to allocate the codeline Returns code line Example s := site() println(site_file(s, _), site_line(s), site_code(s, _)) ---"},{"title":"18. Builtins: 18.4 Miscellaneous","heading":"18.4 Miscellaneous","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#18-4-miscellaneous","text":"18.4 Miscellaneous copy(T[, Region]) -> T Copies a composite value into the destination region. If the final Region argument is omitted, the copy is allocated in the caller's local region. copy also supports uniform function-call syntax: value.copy() and value.copy(out_region) are equivalent to calls with value as their first argument. Supported source types: String Vec[P] Dict[P] Option[P] structs whose each field type is P variants whose every payload type is P Here P means int , double , bool , char , String , or an enum type. Strings inside supported containers and variants are allocated in the destination region. Example names2 := copy(names, out_region) label2 := copy(label) assert(bool) -> void Checks a condition and fails (aborts execution) if the condition is false. Parameters Boolean expression Example v := [10 of 1] assert(len(v) == 10) fail(String[, int]) -> void Marks the current throwing function as failed. See Error Handling . error_msg([Region]) -> String Returns the current error message. Most commonly used inside except . See Error Handling . error_code() -> int Returns the current error code. See Error Handling ."},{"title":"18. Builtins: copy(T[, Region]) -> T","heading":"copy(T[, Region]) -> T","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#copy-t-region-t","text":"copy(T[, Region]) -> T Copies a composite value into the destination region. If the final Region argument is omitted, the copy is allocated in the caller's local region. copy also supports uniform function-call syntax: value.copy() and value.copy(out_region) are equivalent to calls with value as their first argument. Supported source types: String Vec[P] Dict[P] Option[P] structs whose each field type is P variants whose every payload type is P Here P means int , double , bool , char , String , or an enum type. Strings inside supported containers and variants are allocated in the destination region. Example names2 := copy(names, out_region) label2 := copy(label)"},{"title":"18. Builtins: assert(bool) -> void","heading":"assert(bool) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#assert-bool-void","text":"assert(bool) -> void Checks a condition and fails (aborts execution) if the condition is false. Parameters Boolean expression Example v := [10 of 1] assert(len(v) == 10)"},{"title":"18. Builtins: fail(String[, int]) -> void","heading":"fail(String[, int]) -> void","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#fail-string-int-void","text":"fail(String[, int]) -> void Marks the current throwing function as failed. See Error Handling ."},{"title":"18. Builtins: error_msg([Region]) -> String","heading":"error_msg([Region]) -> String","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#error-msg-region-string","text":"error_msg([Region]) -> String Returns the current error message. Most commonly used inside except . See Error Handling ."},{"title":"18. Builtins: error_code() -> int","heading":"error_code() -> int","url":"overview/18-builtins.html#error-code-int","text":"error_code() -> int Returns the current error code. See Error Handling ."},{"title":"19. Standard library","heading":"19. Standard library","url":"overview/19-standard-library.html#19-standard-library","text":"19. Standard library Jik provides a small but useful standard library. The modules below cover common tasks such as strings, input/output, math, randomness, and testing. jik::std - basic utility functions jik::char - character-related helpers jik::bytes - binary-safe byte sequences and buffers jik::string - string operations jik::strbuf - mutable string buffer support jik::fs - filesystem operations jik::io - file and stream I/O jik::region - region allocator diagnostics jik::rand - pseudo-random number generation jik::sys - system-level utilities jik::process - shell-free process execution jik::argparse - command-line argument parsing jik::path - lexical path utilities jik::math - mathematical functions and constants jik::testing - support for writing tests These modules are imported in the same way as other Jik modules, but using paths under jik/... . For example: use \"jik/math\" use \"jik/testing\" as test ---"}];
